"""A Level Religious Studies (0785), Lower Sixth: New Testament: Gospel Theology (5 lessons), Alternative Track: Early Church History or
Philosophy of Religion (4 lessons) and Examination (Paper 1, Paper 2, Paper 3: 3 lessons).

Source: KKEVO's master curriculum blueprint (L6M4, L6M5) and the Board's 2023 Advanced Level report for Religious Studies (paraphrased).
The lessons are descriptive and scholarly: they say what Christians believe, what scholars argue and where scholars disagree. Scripture is
paraphrased and chapters and verses are cited only where they are standard. Dates for the early Church are the usual textbook dates; where
textbooks differ the lesson says "check your textbook". Nothing here is quoted from a past paper.
"""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("religious-studies", "lower-sixth")

M4 = "New Testament: Gospel Theology"
M5 = "Alternative Track: Early Church History or Philosophy of Religion"
MX = "Examination"

TRACK = ("**Which track does your school teach?** The Board's 2023 report names these sections for Paper 3: the Gospel of Luke, John, Acts and "
         "First Corinthians, Philosophy of Religion, Ethics and Morality and the Historical Development of Islam. It does not name Early Church "
         "History as a section of its own, so check with your teacher which track (Early Church History or Philosophy of Religion) your school "
         "teaches and which parts of it your Paper 2 and Paper 3 questions will draw on.")

LESSONS = []


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Christological titles
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M4, topic="Christological titles",
    title="Christological titles: Messiah, Son of God, Son of Man and Logos",
    slug="rsl2-christological-titles",
    minutes=24,
    summary="The titles the Gospels give Jesus (Messiah, Son of God, Son of Man, Logos): their Jewish and Greek background, where each Gospel uses them, what scholars debate, and how to write about them in an essay.",
    objectives=[
        "Define Christology and a Christological title, and explain why the titles matter for the study of the Gospels",
        "Explain the background and meaning of Messiah, Son of God, Son of Man and Logos",
        "Say which Gospels stress which title and give an example of each from the Gospel text",
        "Present scholarly debate (for example about the Son of Man sayings) fairly and evaluate it",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Christology", "Messiah (Christ)", "Son of God", "Son of Man", "Logos", "Messianic secret", "High and low Christology"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When people in a market in Kumba say 'chief', 'elder' or 'doctor', each word tells you what role the person has and what you may expect from him. In the same way, the Gospels use **titles** to say who Jesus is. A title is a short answer to the question Jesus himself is said to have asked in Mark 8: 'Who do you say that I am?'"),
        T("**Christology** is the study of the person and work of Jesus Christ: who he is and what he does. The Gospels do not give one title only. Each Gospel writer chooses, shapes and sometimes avoids titles to show his own picture of Jesus. In this lesson we describe four main titles and ask what each meant to its first hearers. We write as scholars: we say what Christians believe and what scholars argue, and we do not decide between them."),
        D("A **Christological title** is a name or description given to Jesus in the New Testament (for example Messiah, Son of God, Son of Man, Lord, Logos) that expresses a belief about his identity or role.", title="Definition"),
        H("Messiah (Christ)"),
        D("**Messiah** comes from a Hebrew word meaning 'anointed one'. The Greek translation is **Christos**, from which we get 'Christ'. In the Old Testament kings, priests and sometimes prophets were anointed with oil to mark them as chosen by God.", title="Messiah and Christ"),
        T("In the time of Jesus, Jewish hopes about a coming anointed one were not all the same. Many expected a descendant of King David who would free Israel from foreign rule and restore God's kingdom. Others looked for a priestly figure, or a prophet. Scholars stress that there was no single fixed expectation, so you should avoid the sentence 'all Jews expected a warrior king'."),
        T("In **Mark** the turning point is Peter's confession at Caesarea Philippi (Mark 8:27-30). Peter says that Jesus is the Christ, and Jesus tells the disciples not to tell anyone. Then he begins to teach that the Son of Man must suffer. Mark thus joins the title 'Messiah' to suffering, not to military victory. The Jewish leaders' question at the trial uses the same title (Mark 14:61-62)."),
        T("Scholars use the phrase **messianic secret**, introduced by William Wrede in 1901, for Mark's repeated pattern in which Jesus silences demons and sometimes people he has healed. Wrede argued that this was a way for the early Church to explain why Jesus was not recognised as Messiah during his life. Other scholars suggest that Jesus himself avoided the title because it could lead to political misunderstanding. This is a good example of a debate to present without choosing a winner."),
        T("In **John** the title is stated openly early on. John the Baptist says plainly that he himself is not the Christ (John 1:20), and Andrew tells his brother that they have found the Messiah (John 1:41). The writer says his aim is to lead readers to believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God (John 20:31)."),
        H("Son of God"),
        T("In the Old Testament 'son of God' is used of the nation Israel, of the king in the royal Psalms, and sometimes of angelic beings. It stresses a special relationship and obedience, not physical descent. In the Greek world, rulers and wonder-workers were sometimes called 'sons of gods'. Gospel readers in different places could therefore hear the title in different ways."),
        T("In **Mark** the title is used at the baptism (a heavenly voice calls Jesus 'my beloved Son'), at the transfiguration and in the centurion's words at the cross (Mark 15:39). The first verse of the Gospel in many (not all) manuscripts already reads 'Son of God'. Mark seems to let the reader know who Jesus is before most characters can see it. **Matthew** and **Luke** add the birth stories, which say Jesus' sonship begins with the work of the Holy Spirit. In **John** the Son has a unique, close relationship with the Father, and Jesus speaks of the Father more than in any other Gospel."),
        KEY("Messiah and Son of God compared", "Do not treat the two titles as the same. In first-century Jewish usage 'Son of God' was a possible title for a king, but in the Gospels it often says more about the closeness of Jesus to God.", cards=[
            ["Messiah", "A Jewish title for God's chosen, anointed agent. Linked to David's line and to the hope for God's rule. Mark joins it to suffering."],
            ["Son of God", "A title of closeness to God and obedience. Used for Israel and for kings, and in the Gospels also for a unique relationship with the Father."],
        ]),
        H("Son of Man"),
        T("This is the title most often used in the Gospels and almost only by Jesus himself, who uses it about himself. The Greek phrase is awkward, which suggests it translates an Aramaic expression. In Aramaic and Hebrew 'son of man' can simply mean 'a human being' or be a roundabout way of saying 'I'. In **Daniel 7:13-14** a figure 'like a son of man' comes with the clouds of heaven and receives everlasting rule. Later Jewish writings also use the figure, and scholars debate how developed the idea was in Jesus' day."),
        T("Scholars (following a classification often attributed to Rudolf Bultmann) usually sort the sayings into three groups: sayings about the Son of Man's **present activity** (for example, he has authority to forgive sins, Mark 2:10, or he is lord of the Sabbath, Mark 2:28), sayings about his **suffering, death and rising** (Mark 8:31; 9:31; 10:33-34), and sayings about his **future coming in glory** (Mark 13:26; 14:62). Whether Jesus himself used all three types, or the Church created some of them, is disputed. Check your textbook for the views you are expected to know."),
        D("**Son of Man**: a title used in the Gospels almost always by Jesus about himself. It may carry the sense of 'human being', the suffering servant who dies for others, and the heavenly figure of Daniel 7 who is given authority.", title="Son of Man"),
        H("Logos (the Word)"),
        T("The Prologue of John (John 1:1-18) calls Jesus the **Logos**, a Greek word usually translated 'Word' (it also carries the senses of reason, speech and plan). The writer says the Word was with God, was God and became flesh. Two backgrounds help explain it. In the **Old Testament**, God creates by speaking, the 'word of the Lord' comes to the prophets, and Wisdom is described as present with God at creation. In the **Greek world**, philosophers such as the Stoics used logos for the reason that orders the universe, and the Jewish writer Philo of Alexandria used it to speak of God's agent in creation. Scholars disagree about which background mattered most to John; many think the Jewish background is primary. John goes beyond both: the Word became a human being, which no philosopher had said."),
        T("Notice that John does not use the title again after the Prologue. Instead he works with the same idea by the titles Son and Son of God, and by the 'I am' sayings (see the lesson on the Gospel of John)."),
        H("High and low Christology"),
        D("**High Christology** stresses the divinity of Jesus and his pre-existence (John is the usual example). **Low Christology** stresses his humanity, his role as a prophet or teacher and his dependence on God (parts of Mark, Luke and Acts are sometimes described this way). The terms describe emphasis, not a quarrel between Gospels.", title="High and low Christology"),
        EX("Worked example 1: matching a title to a passage", intro="Question (written for practice): 'The centurion at the cross said, Truly this man was the Son of God.' Which Gospel is this, which title is it, and why might Mark have placed it there?", steps=[
            S("Identify", text="The saying is in Mark 15:39. The title is Son of God."),
            S("Place in the Gospel", text="It comes at the death of Jesus, after the Gospel has shown him as a suffering figure."),
            S("Explain", text="A Roman soldier, an outsider, is the first human in the narrative to say it openly after Jesus' death, which fits Mark's theme that Jesus is recognised as Son of God through the cross."),
            S("Evaluate", text="Some scholars say the Greek can mean 'a son of a god' in a Roman mouth; others say Mark means the full Christian sense. Present both."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: planning an essay", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Examine the ways in which the Gospels use the title Son of Man.' Plan a response.", steps=[
            S("Introduction", text="Define the title, say it is almost always on Jesus' lips and that it appears in all four Gospels."),
            S("Background", text="Daniel 7 and the plain sense 'human being'. Note that scholars disagree on how fixed the Jewish background was."),
            S("Three groups of sayings", text="Present activity (Mark 2:10, 28), suffering (Mark 8:31) and future coming (Mark 14:62), each with a brief explanation."),
            S("Evaluation", text="Debate over authenticity: did Jesus use each group? Show the strength of one view and its weakness."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The title joins authority, suffering and final vindication."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Treating 'Son of Man' as a title for the humanity and 'Son of God' as a title for the divinity of Jesus", "This is a common simple rule but it is too simple. Son of Man also refers to a heavenly figure (Daniel 7), and Son of God had meanings in the Old Testament that were not about divinity. Say what each title meant in its background first."),
        MISTAKE("Telling the story instead of explaining the title", "The Board's report on the Gospel of John repeatedly says that candidates narrate events and do not explain what the Gospel says about the person of Jesus. When a question asks about identity or a title, name the title, give the text, then explain what it means and how the writer uses it."),
        QUIZ("Which Gospel title did Peter use at Caesarea Philippi in Mark 8?", ["Son of Man", "Christ (Messiah)", "Logos", "Lord of the Sabbath"], 1,
             "Peter says that Jesus is the Christ, the Greek form of Messiah (Mark 8:29).", hint="Think of the title that means 'anointed one'.",
             feedback=["Son of Man is the title Jesus uses next, when he teaches that the Son of Man must suffer.", "", "Logos is the term of the Prologue of John, not Peter's confession.", "Lord of the Sabbath is a description in Mark 2:28, not Peter's confession."]),
        QUIZ("Which statement about the Logos is best?", ["It appears in the Prologue of John and may combine Old Testament and Greek ideas", "It is used by Jesus as his main title in the Synoptic Gospels", "It is a Hebrew word for the Temple", "It means 'king' in Greek"], 0,
             "The Prologue (John 1:1-18) uses Logos, and scholars debate whether the Jewish Word and Wisdom ideas or Greek philosophy were behind it.", hint="Which Gospel begins with 'In the beginning'?",
             feedback=["", "Jesus does not use Logos as a title in the Synoptics.", "Logos is not a word for the Temple.", "Logos means word, reason or plan, not 'king'."]),
        PQ("Explain what the title 'Messiah' meant in Jewish thought of the first century and how Mark connects it to suffering. (Model answer.)",
           "Messiah means 'anointed one'; kings and priests were anointed in the Old Testament. In Jesus' time hopes varied: many looked for a descendant of David who would restore God's rule, but no single expectation was universal. In Mark, Peter confesses Jesus as the Christ (8:29). Jesus tells the disciples to keep quiet and teaches that the Son of Man must suffer, be rejected and rise (8:31). Mark thus redefines the title: Jesus is Messiah as one who serves and suffers. The messianic secret (Wrede) and the question at the trial (14:61-62) also show Mark's interest in how the title is revealed. Scholars debate why Mark includes the secret, so present both views."),
        PQ("Outline how the title Son of Man is used in the Gospels and give one debate. (Model answer.)",
           "The title is used almost only by Jesus about himself. Background: Daniel 7:13-14 and the ordinary sense 'human being'. Three groups of sayings: present authority (Mark 2:10, 28), suffering (Mark 8:31) and future coming (Mark 14:62). Debate: whether Jesus himself used all three groups. Some scholars think the future sayings come from Jesus and the others from the Church, others the reverse; some argue the phrase was only a way of speaking about himself. A good answer names the groups, gives one text from each, and explains the debate without claiming the matter is settled."),
        PQ("'John's presentation of Jesus is the highest in the New Testament.' Discuss with reference to the Logos. (Plan.)",
           "Introduce high and low Christology. For: the Prologue says the Word was with God and was God (1:1) and became flesh (1:14); pre-existence; the 'I am' sayings. Against or qualify: Mark also calls Jesus Son of God at the beginning and end; Paul's letters (earlier) already use very high language; John also stresses the humanity of Jesus (tired at the well, weeps at Lazarus' tomb). Evaluate: John states openly what the Synoptics suggest; the difference is more one of emphasis. Conclude with a clear judgement."),
        TIP("Exam link: titles can appear as Paper 1 multiple choice (identify the title from a description) and in Paper 3 structured questions on the Gospels. The Board's report says candidates found the question on the identity of Jesus at the beginning of John's Gospel hard because they retold events instead of using facts from the text, such as John the Baptist's denial that he is the Christ. Use named titles and verses in your answers."),
        SUM("A Christological title expresses a belief about who Jesus is; each Gospel chooses and shapes titles", "Messiah means 'anointed one'; Mark links it to suffering and Wrede's 'messianic secret' is debated", "Son of God can describe Israel or a king; in the Gospels it often points to a unique closeness to the Father", "Son of Man: almost always Jesus' own phrase; Daniel 7; three groups of sayings; scholars debate their origin", "Logos (John 1:1-18): Old Testament Word and Wisdom, and Greek logos, with the addition that the Word became flesh", "Write what Christians believe and what scholars argue, and do not state debated points as fact"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Kingdom of God
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M4, topic="The Kingdom of God",
    title="The Kingdom of God: present, future and the call to respond",
    slug="rsl2-kingdom-of-god",
    minutes=23,
    summary="The central theme of Jesus' preaching in the Synoptic Gospels: its Old Testament background, its meaning as God's rule, the debate over present and future, and its demands and signs.",
    objectives=[
        "Define the Kingdom of God as God's rule and place it in its Old Testament and Jewish background",
        "Describe how the Synoptic Gospels present the Kingdom as both present and future",
        "Summarise the scholarly positions: futurist, realised and inaugurated",
        "Explain the demands of the Kingdom and its signs (teaching, healing, welcome to outsiders) with examples",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Kingdom of God", "Basileia", "Realised eschatology", "Inaugurated eschatology", "Repentance", "Kingdom of heaven"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Think of a village in the North West Region where a new chief is installed. Before the ceremony people say 'the chief is coming'. After it they say 'the chief is here, and his rule has begun'. But even then some of what he promises takes years to happen. A good picture of the Kingdom of God in the Gospels is somewhat like that: God's rule is announced, has begun, and is still to be completed."),
        T("The Synoptic Gospels (Matthew, Mark and Luke) describe the preaching of Jesus as being above all about the Kingdom of God. Mark summarises it in a single saying: the time is fulfilled, the Kingdom of God has come near, so repent and believe the good news (Mark 1:15). Many scholars regard the Kingdom as the centre of the message of Jesus, and the Board's syllabus names it as its own topic."),
        D("The **Kingdom of God** (Greek **basileia**) is the rule or reign of God: God acting as king in the world and in human lives. The word describes God's active kingship more than a place on a map. **Matthew** usually writes 'kingdom of heaven', probably out of Jewish reverence in avoiding the name of God; the meaning is the same.", title="Definition"),
        H("Background: God as king in the Old Testament"),
        T("The idea did not begin with Jesus. In the Old Testament God is praised as king over Israel and over all creation (for example in many Psalms). When Israel had human kings, prophets reminded them that God was the true king. After the exile, when Israel was ruled by foreign powers, many Jews hoped that God would act decisively to establish his rule, to defeat evil, and to restore Israel. The book of Daniel presents God's kingdom as one that will outlast all earthly empires (Daniel 2 and 7)."),
        T("When Jesus said that the Kingdom was near, his hearers would have understood this hope. What was new in his preaching was the claim that God's rule was already breaking in through his own words and deeds."),
        H("Present and future"),
        T("The Gospels speak of the Kingdom in two ways. Some sayings and parables speak of a Kingdom **already at work**: when Jesus casts out demons by the finger or Spirit of God, then the Kingdom of God has come upon them (Luke 11:20; Matthew 12:28). Others speak of a Kingdom **yet to come**, for example the prayer 'your kingdom come' (Matthew 6:10), the Son of Man coming with power (Mark 13), and the great feast where many come from east and west (Matthew 8:11)."),
        T("Luke 17:20-21 is often discussed. Jesus says that the Kingdom does not come with signs to be observed and that it is 'within' or 'among' you. The Greek can be translated either way. Most scholars today prefer 'among you', meaning present in the ministry of Jesus, but check your textbook."),
        KEY("Three main scholarly positions", "Know all three and be able to name one scholar for each. Many textbooks give these.", cards=[
            ["Futurist (consistent eschatology)", "Albert Schweitzer argued that Jesus expected God to end the present age soon and bring the Kingdom in power. Present sayings are about the Kingdom's approach."],
            ["Realised eschatology", "C. H. Dodd argued that the Kingdom had already come in the ministry of Jesus and that future sayings are pictures of its fullness."],
            ["Inaugurated ('already but not yet')", "Many later scholars (for example Joachim Jeremias, W. G. Kummel) argue that the Kingdom has begun in Jesus' ministry and will be completed in the future. This is the most widely held view."],
        ]),
        D("**Eschatology** is the study of the 'last things': the end of the age, God's final judgement and the completion of his rule. **Realised eschatology** says the end is already present; **inaugurated eschatology** says it has begun and is not yet complete.", title="Eschatology"),
        H("The Kingdom in the parables"),
        T("Many parables begin with the words 'the Kingdom of God is like...'. The Sower (Mark 4) describes the different receptions of the word. The Mustard Seed and the Yeast (Matthew 13; Luke 13) show a very small beginning with a great result. The Growing Seed (Mark 4:26-29) stresses that God's rule grows by his own power. The Great Banquet (Luke 14; compare Matthew 22) shows an invitation that is refused by some and accepted by unexpected guests. The next lesson takes parables as a topic in itself; here notice how they show the Kingdom as a gift, as a surprise and as a call for a response."),
        H("The demands and signs of the Kingdom"),
        T("**Repentance and faith**: Mark 1:15 joins the announcement to a call to turn around and trust. **Discipleship and cost**: the Gospels say that following Jesus may cost family ties, possessions and even life. **Righteousness and love**: the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5-7) shows the lifestyle of those under God's rule, and love of God and neighbour is the summary of the law (Mark 12:28-34). **Childlike acceptance**: Mark 10:14-15 says that the Kingdom belongs to those like children."),
        T("The signs of the Kingdom are in what Jesus does. He heals the sick, casts out demons, forgives sins and eats with tax collectors and sinners. When John the Baptist asks from prison whether Jesus is the one to come, Jesus answers by pointing to the blind who see, the lame who walk and the poor who hear good news (Luke 7:18-23; Matthew 11:2-6). In **Luke** especially the good news is for the poor, women, Samaritans and outcasts."),
        EX("Worked example 1: present and future in one passage", intro="Question (written for practice): Show that the Gospels speak of the Kingdom as both present and future.", steps=[
            S("Present", text="Luke 11:20: if Jesus drives out demons by the finger of God, the Kingdom has already come upon them. Mark 1:15: the Kingdom has come near."),
            S("Future", text="The Lord's Prayer asks 'your kingdom come' (Matthew 6:10, also Luke 11:2). Mark 13 and Mark 14:62 speak of a future coming of the Son of Man."),
            S("Explain the tension", text="The two can be joined: the Kingdom has begun in Jesus' ministry and will be completed by God."),
            S("Scholars", text="Schweitzer stressed the future, Dodd the present, and many now say 'already but not yet'. Give one criticism of each if the question asks for evaluation."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: the signs of the Kingdom", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain why healings and meals are important to the Kingdom in Luke.", steps=[
            S("Point", text="They are signs that God's rule is breaking in."),
            S("Evidence 1", text="Luke 7:22: the blind see, the lame walk, the poor hear good news. This answers whether Jesus is the one to come."),
            S("Evidence 2", text="Jesus eats with tax collectors and sinners (Luke 5:27-32; 15:1-2). The meal is welcome and restoration of outcasts, which matches the parables of the Lost Sheep and the Lost Son."),
            S("Link", text="The Kingdom is therefore not only teaching but action: the lives of the poor and the excluded are changed."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying that the Kingdom of God means heaven or only 'life after death'", "In the Gospels it is first of all the rule of God, announced as arriving in the ministry of Jesus. Later hopes for a future completion are included, but a one-word answer 'heaven' misses the meaning."),
        MISTAKE("Giving only one scholar's view as if it settled the matter", "The Kingdom's timing is debated. Give at least two positions, say what texts support each, and say which you find more convincing and why. Do not call a scholarly theory a fact."),
        QUIZ("Which view says the Kingdom has begun in the ministry of Jesus and will be completed in the future?", ["Futurist eschatology", "Realised eschatology", "Inaugurated eschatology", "Apocalyptic eschatology only"], 2,
             "'Already but not yet' is the inaugurated view, held by many scholars.", hint="Which view combines both present and future?",
             feedback=["The futurist view stresses the future Kingdom only.", "Realised eschatology says the Kingdom has already come.", "", "Apocalyptic is a type of Jewish writing, not the name of this view."]),
        QUIZ("Why does Matthew write 'kingdom of heaven'?", ["It is a different kingdom from Mark's", "Probably out of Jewish reverence in avoiding the divine name", "Because Matthew thinks the Kingdom is only in the sky", "Because Luke used it first"], 1,
             "Most scholars say Matthew's readers were largely Jewish and 'heaven' is a respectful way to speak of God.", hint="Think of Jewish customs about the name of God.",
             feedback=["Matthew and Mark mean the same.", "", "The phrase does not mean a place in the sky only.", "Luke uses 'kingdom of God', as Mark does."]),
        PQ("Explain what is meant by the Kingdom of God in the preaching of Jesus. (Model answer.)",
           "It is God's rule or reign (Greek basileia), not mainly a territory. The Old Testament speaks of God as king, and in the post-exilic period many hoped God would act to establish his rule. Jesus announces that it has come near (Mark 1:15) and shows it at work in his healings and exorcisms (Luke 11:20). Some sayings describe a future completion (the Lord's Prayer, Mark 13). It calls for repentance and faith, and its signs are healing, forgiveness and welcome to the poor and outcasts. Scholars debate the balance of present and future."),
        PQ("Compare the views of Dodd and Schweitzer on the Kingdom. (Model answer.)",
           "Schweitzer (consistent eschatology): Jesus expected God to bring in the Kingdom soon, as a dramatic end of the present age; the Kingdom's coming is future. Dodd (realised eschatology): the Kingdom has already come in Jesus' ministry, and future sayings use pictures to describe a present reality. Strengths: Schweitzer takes the Jewish apocalyptic background and Mark 13 seriously; Dodd takes Luke 11:20 and Luke 17:21 seriously. Weaknesses: each has to explain away sayings that point the other way. The widely held 'already but not yet' view tries to keep both."),
        PQ("'The Kingdom of God is mainly about the poor and outcasts.' Discuss for Luke. (Plan.)",
           "For: Luke 4:16-21 (good news to the poor), Luke 6:20-21 (beatitudes), Luke 7:22, Luke 14:12-24 (the banquet for the poor and disabled), Luke 15 (lost sheep, coin and son), Zacchaeus (Luke 19). Against or qualify: Luke also warns the rich who refuse to respond (Luke 16; Luke 18:18-25) but includes rich people who respond (Zacchaeus), and the Kingdom also demands repentance and faith from everyone. Conclude: the poor and outcasts are prominent, but the Kingdom is an invitation to all who respond."),
        TIP("Exam link: the Kingdom appears in Paper 1 (what is meant by the term, which scholar held which view) and in Paper 3 structured questions on Luke. The Board's report praises answers that situate teaching in its Palestinian setting; use the Jewish background of God as king."),
        SUM("The Kingdom of God is God's rule, announced by Jesus as near (Mark 1:15)", "Background: God as king in the Old Testament and hopes after the exile", "Present (Luke 11:20) and future (the Lord's Prayer, Mark 13): futurist, realised and inaugurated views", "Parables show it as gift, surprise and growth", "Demands: repentance, faith, discipleship, love; signs: healing, forgiveness and welcome to outcasts", "Matthew's 'kingdom of heaven' means the same as 'kingdom of God'"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Parables and miracles
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M4, topic="Parables and miracles",
    title="Parables and miracles in the Gospels: forms, meaning and interpretation",
    slug="rsl2-parables-and-miracles",
    minutes=25,
    summary="What parables and miracles are, how scholars classify and interpret them, what well-known examples (Good Samaritan, Prodigal Son, Sower; healings and nature miracles) teach, and how to answer questions that ask about faith and response.",
    objectives=[
        "Define parable and miracle and give the main types and features of each",
        "Summarise how scholars interpret parables (Julicher, Dodd, Jeremias) and miracles (signs of the Kingdom, the form-critical pattern, debates about history)",
        "Explain the teaching of at least three parables and three miracles with the Gospel references",
        "Link both to the Kingdom of God and to faith, and avoid retelling the story without explaining it",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Parable (mashal)", "Allegory", "Miracle", "Sign (semeion)", "Power (dunamis)", "Exorcism", "Faith", "Form-critical pattern"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A teacher in Bafoussam who wants to explain fairness may tell a story about two farmers sharing a stream instead of giving a definition. The story is remembered long after a lecture is forgotten. Jesus is shown by the Gospels as a teller of such stories (**parables**) and as one who did powerful works (**miracles**). Both point to the Kingdom of God, and both ask a response from the hearer."),
        T("This lesson has two parts: first parables, then miracles. In each part we ask the same three questions: what is it, how do scholars understand it, and what does it teach in the Gospels? The Board's report on the Gospel of Luke says that many candidates narrated the parable of the Good Samaritan instead of explaining its teaching on love and discipleship. Keep that warning in mind throughout."),
        H("Part 1: Parables"),
        D("A **parable** (Greek parabole; Hebrew mashal) is a short story or comparison from daily life, told to make a point about God, the Kingdom or the right way to live. It often has an unexpected turn that challenges the hearer.", title="Definition"),
        T("Parables make up a large part of the teaching of Jesus in the Synoptic Gospels. They are not found in John's Gospel, which uses long speeches and symbolic sayings instead (compare the 'I am' sayings). The settings are familiar to Galilean life: farming, fishing, weddings, debts, households and travel on a dangerous road. In Cameroon today you could compare this with proverbs and stories told by elders: ordinary images making a serious point."),
        KEY("Kinds of parable", "Textbooks group parables in different ways. A simple grouping that is accepted by many scholars:", cards=[
            ["Kingdom parables", "Describe what God's rule is like: the Sower, Mustard Seed, Yeast, Growing Seed (Mark 4; Matthew 13)."],
            ["Parables of grace and forgiveness", "The Lost Sheep, Lost Coin and Lost (Prodigal) Son (Luke 15) and the Unforgiving Servant (Matthew 18)."],
            ["Parables of response and discipleship", "The Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37), the Talents (Matthew 25), the Two Builders (Matthew 7)."],
            ["Parables of judgement and readiness", "The Ten Bridesmaids (Matthew 25), the Rich Fool (Luke 12), the Rich Man and Lazarus (Luke 16)."],
        ]),
        H("How have scholars interpreted parables?"),
        T("In the early Church and the Middle Ages many parables were read as **allegories**, in which each detail stands for something else (for example, in the Good Samaritan, the inn is the Church). Adolf Julicher (late nineteenth century) argued strongly against this: each parable, he said, has one main point and is not an allegory. C. H. Dodd stressed that parables must be read in the situation of Jesus' ministry as pictures of the Kingdom now at work. Joachim Jeremias tried to recover the original setting of each parable and said that the Church later added allegorical applications (for example, in the explanation of the Sower in Mark 4:13-20). Later scholars note that some parables (such as the Sower or the Wicked Tenants in Mark 12) do have more than one point."),
        T("Mark 4:10-12 says that parables were told so that those outside would see but not understand. This is a difficult passage, and scholars give different explanations: some say it describes the result of the parables, not their purpose; some say it is the Church's later explanation; others link it to the prophet Isaiah. Check your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25-37)", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain the teaching of the parable of the Good Samaritan.", steps=[
            S("Setting", text="A lawyer asks what he must do to inherit eternal life, then asks 'who is my neighbour?' (10:29)."),
            S("The story", text="A man is attacked on the road from Jerusalem to Jericho. A priest and a Levite pass by. A Samaritan, a person Jews often despised, helps him, pays and returns."),
            S("The turn", text="Jesus changes the question: not 'who is my neighbour?' but 'who proved to be a neighbour?' (10:36). The one who showed mercy."),
            S("Teaching", text="Love for neighbour is shown in action and does not stop at the borders of race, religion or class. Luke places it just after the love commandment: love of God and neighbour is the way of discipleship."),
            S("Link to the Kingdom", text="The parable shows the reversal typical of Luke: the outsider shows what God's people should be."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: the Lost Son (Luke 15:11-32)", intro="Question (written for practice): What does the parable of the Lost Son teach about God and about the response of people?", steps=[
            S("Setting", text="Told after the Pharisees complain that Jesus welcomes sinners and eats with them (Luke 15:1-2); it is the third of three 'lost' parables."),
            S("Younger son", text="Asks his share, wastes it, returns in need. The father runs to meet him and restores him. Teaching: God's welcome of the repentant."),
            S("Older son", text="Resents the feast. The parable ends with the father's invitation and without the son's answer. Teaching: a challenge to those, like the Pharisees, who object to God's mercy."),
            S("Evaluation", text="Some scholars take the parable as having one main point (the father's love); others see two points (both sons)."),
        ]),
        H("Part 2: Miracles"),
        D("A **miracle** in the Gospels is an extraordinary act, such as a healing, an exorcism, a nature miracle or a raising of the dead, which the Gospels present as the work of God through Jesus. The Synoptics often call them **dunameis** (powers); John calls them **semeia** (signs).", title="Definition"),
        KEY("Types of miracle", "A standard grouping, with examples:", cards=[
            ["Healings", "A leper (Mark 1:40-45), the paralysed man (Mark 2:1-12), the woman with bleeding (Mark 5:25-34), the ten lepers (Luke 17:11-19)."],
            ["Exorcisms", "The man in the synagogue (Mark 1:21-28), the Gerasene man (Mark 5:1-20)."],
            ["Nature miracles", "Calming the storm (Mark 4:35-41), the feeding of the five thousand (Mark 6:30-44), walking on water (Mark 6:45-52)."],
            ["Raisings from the dead", "Jairus' daughter (Mark 5:21-43), the widow's son at Nain (Luke 7:11-17); Lazarus in John 11."],
        ]),
        T("What do the miracles mean in the Gospels? First, they are **signs of the Kingdom**: when Jesus drives out demons the Kingdom of God has come (Luke 11:20). Second, they show his **authority** over illness, evil and nature, which leads to questions about his identity (Who is this, that even the wind and sea obey him? Mark 4:41). Third, they show his **compassion**, particularly to people whom society had pushed aside. Fourth, they are bound up with **faith**: in Mark 5:34 and Mark 10:52 Jesus says that the faith of the person has made them well, though in Mark 6:5-6 he is described as unable to do many works in Nazareth because of the people's unbelief. The Board's report says that a question on Luke's miracles asked whether the recipients believed in them as genuine, and that this was easy to miss. Always say whether and how the people in the story respond with faith, wonder, fear or rejection."),
        T("Form critics (see the lesson on oral tradition) found that many healing stories follow a pattern: the problem is described, there is an appeal, Jesus speaks or touches, the cure is stated, and the crowd responds. Scholars use the pattern to ask how the stories were told in the early Church, not to deny that they describe events."),
        H("Debates about miracles"),
        T("Believers hold that the miracles show God's power at work in Jesus. Some scholars treat the reports of healings and exorcisms as most likely to go back to the ministry of Jesus, because they are so widely present in the sources, while holding different views on the nature miracles. Other scholars, following the eighteenth-century philosopher David Hume, argue that miracles are highly improbable by definition, and that the stories grew in the telling. Others, such as Rudolf Bultmann, proposed that the stories should be understood for their meaning for faith. Note that these are positions people hold, and an examination answer should present them with their reasons and critics."),
        MISTAKE("Retelling a parable or a miracle from start to finish", "The Board's report says that candidates answering on the Good Samaritan wrote mostly narration and gave too little attention to love and discipleship. A short summary of two or three sentences is enough. Spend most of the answer on the meaning, the setting in the Gospel and the response asked from the hearer."),
        MISTAKE("Saying every parable is an allegory, or none is", "Both extremes are poor. Say that some interpreters allegorise, that Julicher insisted on one main point, and that today many allow some parables more than one point. Give a parable as an example."),
        QUIZ("What Greek word does John use for miracles?", ["Dunamis", "Semeion (sign)", "Parabole", "Basileia"], 1,
             "John calls the miracles signs (semeia) that point to the identity of Jesus; the Synoptics often use dunamis (power).", hint="Think of the Gospel that has seven 'signs'.",
             feedback=["Dunamis (power) is the Synoptic term.", "", "Parabole means parable.", "Basileia means kingdom or reign."]),
        QUIZ("Which scholar argued that each parable normally has a single main point and is not an allegory?", ["C. H. Dodd", "Adolf Julicher", "William Wrede", "Rudolf Bultmann"], 1,
             "Julicher's reaction against allegorical reading is a standard point in textbooks.", hint="He wrote in the late nineteenth century.",
             feedback=["Dodd linked parables to the Kingdom now at work.", "", "Wrede is known for the messianic secret.", "Bultmann is known for form criticism and demythologising."]),
        PQ("Explain how the parable of the Prodigal Son illustrates the Kingdom of God. (Model answer.)",
           "Told to the Pharisees who criticised Jesus for eating with sinners (Luke 15:1-2). The father's welcome of the returning younger son shows God's mercy toward the repentant; the older son's resentment mirrors the Pharisees' attitude. The parable shows that the Kingdom is a gift (the son does nothing to deserve the feast), that it calls for repentance and that it includes the outcast. Evaluate: the open ending asks the hearer to respond."),
        PQ("Discuss the view that the miracles of Jesus are best understood as signs of the Kingdom. (Plan.)",
           "For: Luke 11:20 (exorcisms and the Kingdom), Luke 7:22 (the list of healings answers John the Baptist), the Kingdom's reversal of illness and evil. Add: authority (Mark 4:41), compassion (Mark 1:41), faith (Mark 5:34), response of the crowd. Against or qualify: in John the miracles are signs of the identity of Jesus; some scholars say miracle stories were shaped by the Church (form criticism); Hume-type objections; in Mark the miracles do not always lead to faith (Mark 3:22 suggests that some said his power came from Beelzebul). Conclude with a judgement."),
        PQ("Outline the Gospel of Luke's presentation of the response of people to the miracles. (Model answer.)",
           "Respond with wonder and praise (Luke 5:26; 7:16, where the crowd glorified God), with faith (the centurion at Capernaum, Luke 7:1-10; the woman with bleeding, Luke 8:48), with gratitude (the one Samaritan among the ten lepers, Luke 17:11-19), or with fear and rejection (the Gerasenes, Luke 8:37). Luke often shows the faith of outsiders. Note that not everyone believes: some opponents question the source of Jesus' power (Luke 11:15)."),
        TIP("Exam link: parables and miracles are likely subjects for Paper 3 (Luke and John sections) and for Paper 1 identification questions. The Board's report on Luke shows candidates are expected to focus on meaning and response: love and discipleship in the Good Samaritan, and whether the recipients of miracles believed."),
        SUM("A parable is a short story from daily life that makes a point about God, the Kingdom or discipleship; the Synoptics have many, John has none", "Interpreters: allegory (old), one main point (Julicher), Kingdom setting (Dodd), original setting (Jeremias)", "Miracles are healings, exorcisms, nature miracles and raisings, called dunameis (Synoptics) or semeia (John)", "They are signs of the Kingdom, show authority and compassion, and are connected to faith", "Scholars differ on history; present views with reasons", "In answers: explain meaning and response, and keep the story summary short"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. The Gospel of John
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M4, topic="The Gospel of John",
    title="The Gospel of John: purpose, signs, 'I am' sayings and themes",
    slug="rsl2-gospel-of-john",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The Fourth Gospel: authorship and date, purpose and audience, how it differs from the Synoptics, the Prologue, the seven signs, the 'I am' sayings, and the key themes of light, life and belief.",
    objectives=[
        "Discuss the authorship, date, purpose and audience of John and state where scholars disagree",
        "List the differences between John and the Synoptic Gospels and say why they matter",
        "Explain the Prologue, the seven signs and the 'I am' sayings with references",
        "Explain the themes of light and spiritual blindness (John 9) and life and resurrection (John 11)",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Fourth Gospel", "Beloved disciple", "Prologue", "Sign (semeion)", "'I am' saying", "Light and darkness", "Belief", "Paraclete"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("If you read Mark and then John, you feel that you have walked from one town to another. Mark is brisk and has short stories. John has long conversations, rich images and no parables. The Board's report treats John (the Fourth Gospel) as its own section of Paper 3, with questions on the purpose of the Gospel and its audience, the identity of Jesus at the beginning of the ministry, Jesus as the light of the world, the raising of Lazarus, the personality and mission of Jesus, and the trials and crucifixion."),
        T("This lesson prepares you for those questions. We work from the text: you must be able to name a passage and say what it shows."),
        H("Authorship, date and purpose"),
        T("The Gospel does not name its author in the main text. It speaks of a 'beloved disciple' (for example John 13:23; 21:20-24). Church tradition from the second century identified the author with John the son of Zebedee, one of the twelve; that tradition is the one the Board's report assumes. Many modern scholars suggest instead a **Johannine community** (a group of followers) that shaped the Gospel over time, perhaps with an editor and a leading figure, the beloved disciple. Check your textbook for the view you are asked to know and be ready to give the arguments for each."),
        T("The usual date is about AD 90 to 100. The earliest known fragment of the Gospel (a small papyrus piece called P52, now in Manchester) is usually dated early in the second century, which shows the Gospel was already circulating by then. Place of writing is uncertain; tradition says Ephesus."),
        D("The writer states his purpose near the end of chapter 20: these things are written so that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that through believing you may have life in his name (John 20:31).", title="Purpose (John 20:31)"),
        T("The **audience** is debated. The Gospel explains Jewish customs and translates Hebrew or Aramaic words, which suggests that some readers were not Jewish. Yet it has deep roots in the Old Testament, discusses feasts such as Passover, Tabernacles and Dedication, and has sharp conflict with some Jewish authorities. The Board's report describes the followers of John as both Jews and Gentiles. A good answer says that the Gospel addresses a mixed group and probably Christians who were facing opposition and were asking what it means to believe."),
        H("John and the Synoptics"),
        KEY("Main differences", "These are differences of selection and emphasis. Both traditions come from early Christianity and neither is 'wrong'.", cards=[
            ["What John leaves out", "No birth narrative, no parables, no exorcisms, no bread-and-wine words at the Last Supper, no transfiguration."],
            ["What John adds", "Wedding at Cana, Nicodemus, the Samaritan woman, the man born blind, the raising of Lazarus, long farewell speeches (John 13-17), the Paraclete."],
            ["Time and place", "Several journeys to Jerusalem and three Passovers, whereas the Synoptics have one visit to Jerusalem at the end. The Temple cleansing comes early in John (John 2), and late in the Synoptics."],
            ["Language and style", "Long discourses, symbolic images (light, water, bread, shepherd) and the 'I am' sayings."],
        ]),
        H("The Prologue (John 1:1-18)"),
        T("The Prologue is a short poem that introduces the whole Gospel. It says that the Word (Logos) was in the beginning with God, was God and was the agent of creation; that in him was life, and the life was the light of people; that the light shines in darkness and the darkness has not overcome it; that John the Baptist was not the light but a witness to it; and that the Word became flesh and lived among us (John 1:14). The themes of light and darkness, life, witness, belief and rejection are all announced here."),
        T("The Prologue sets John the Baptist beside Jesus in a way that explains the question of Jesus' identity at the start of the ministry. When priests and Levites ask the Baptist who he is, he denies that he is the Christ, Elijah or the prophet (John 1:19-23). He then points to Jesus as the Lamb of God and bears witness that he is the Son of God (John 1:29-34, and see also John 1:49, where Nathanael calls Jesus the Son of God and king of Israel)."),
        H("The seven signs"),
        T("John chooses seven miracles and calls them **signs** (semeia): events that point beyond themselves to who Jesus is. The seven are: water into wine at Cana (2:1-11), the healing of an official's son (4:46-54), the healing of a paralysed man at the pool (5:1-15), the feeding of the five thousand (6:1-14), walking on the water (6:16-21), the healing of the man born blind (9:1-41) and the raising of Lazarus (11:1-44). Each is followed by a conversation or speech that explains what the sign means. The Gospel also says that Jesus did many other signs (20:30), so the seven are a selection."),
        IMG("alrsl2-john-signs-iam.svg", alt="A two-column chart. The seven signs of John: water into wine, official's son, paralysed man, five thousand fed, walking on water, man born blind, Lazarus raised. The seven I am sayings: bread of life, light of the world, door, good shepherd, resurrection and the life, way truth and life, true vine.", caption="Figure 1. The seven signs and the seven 'I am' sayings with a predicate in John."),
        H("The 'I am' sayings"),
        T("In seven sayings Jesus describes himself with a picture, each beginning 'I am': the bread of life (6:35), the light of the world (8:12), the door or gate (10:7, 9), the good shepherd (10:11), the resurrection and the life (11:25), the way, the truth and the life (14:6) and the true vine (15:1). Each links to a sign: the bread of life follows the feeding, the light of the world (chapter 8) is illustrated by the healing of the man born blind (chapter 9), and the resurrection and the life is said before the raising of Lazarus. John also has 'I am' sayings with no picture (for example 8:58), which echo the divine name given to Moses, and some scholars think they claim a share in the name of God."),
        H("Light and spiritual blindness (John 9)"),
        T("Jesus heals a man born blind. The man gradually grows in sight: he first calls Jesus 'the man called Jesus', then a prophet, then, after being driven out, confesses 'Lord, I believe' and worships. At the same time the Pharisees, who claim to see, become more blind in their opposition. Jesus says that he came so that those who do not see may see and those who see may become blind (9:39). The Board's report notes that candidates on this topic only narrated the healing stories and missed the theme of spiritual blindness. Use it: physical sight is a sign of spiritual sight, and refusal to believe is a kind of blindness."),
        H("Life and resurrection (John 11)"),
        T("Lazarus of Bethany is ill and dies. Jesus waits two days, tells the disciples that the illness is for the glory of God, and then comes. To Martha he says 'I am the resurrection and the life' (11:25), and she confesses that he is the Christ, the Son of God. He weeps, calls Lazarus out of the tomb and the man comes out. The sign shows Jesus as giver of life, but also leads to the decision of the leaders to put Jesus to death (11:45-53). It points forward to his own resurrection. Note the difference: Lazarus is raised and will die again; Jesus is raised to a new life."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the purpose of John", intro="Question (written for practice): Discuss the purpose of the Gospel of John and its probable audience.", steps=[
            S("Purpose", text="John 20:31: that readers may believe Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and have life in his name."),
            S("Evidence from the Gospel", text="The signs lead readers to belief; the 'I am' sayings and the Prologue show the identity of Jesus."),
            S("Audience", text="Jews and Gentiles. Evidence: explanation of Jewish customs and translation of words suggests non-Jewish readers; deep Old Testament roots suggest Jewish ones. A community under pressure may have been included."),
            S("Evaluate", text="Some scholars say the purpose is to strengthen those who already believe; others say to persuade outsiders. The Greek of 20:31 allows both."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: Jesus as the light of the world", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain the theme of light in John, with reference to chapter 9.", steps=[
            S("Prologue", text="The Word is the light that shines in darkness (1:4-5)."),
            S("I am saying", text="'I am the light of the world' (8:12), the claim that those who follow will not walk in darkness."),
            S("The sign", text="The man born blind receives physical sight and then spiritual sight (9:35-38), while the Pharisees stay in spiritual blindness (9:39-41)."),
            S("Meaning", text="The Gospel shows belief as sight and unbelief as blindness. Jesus brings judgement by showing who responds."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Narrating the signs without saying what they mean", "In John each sign is a pointer. When asked about the raising of Lazarus, do not retell the whole story: explain 'I am the resurrection and the life', the glory of God, the response of Martha and the leaders' plot, and how it looks forward to Jesus' own resurrection."),
        MISTAKE("Saying that John 'contradicts' the Synoptics", "The Gospels differ in selection, order and style. Many scholars say John knew another tradition, or stressed a different part of the story. Compare them fairly. Do not say that one is false."),
        QUIZ("Which sign is followed by the saying 'I am the resurrection and the life'?", ["The feeding of the five thousand", "The healing of the man born blind", "The raising of Lazarus", "The wedding at Cana"], 2,
             "The saying is spoken to Martha before Lazarus is raised (John 11:25).", hint="Think of Martha and Mary of Bethany.",
             feedback=["The feeding is linked to 'bread of life'.", "The man born blind is linked to 'light of the world'.", "", "Cana is the first sign and has no 'I am' saying attached."]),
        QUIZ("What is the stated purpose of the Gospel of John?", ["To give the family tree of Jesus", "That readers may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and have life", "To list all the parables of Jesus", "To prove that Jerusalem was the capital of Israel"], 1,
             "John 20:31 says this clearly.", hint="It is in chapter 20.",
             feedback=["The genealogies are in Matthew and Luke.", "", "John has no parables.", "John does not argue this."]),
        PQ("Explain the importance of the Prologue (John 1:1-18) for understanding the Gospel. (Model answer.)",
           "The Prologue states in advance who Jesus is: the Word who was with God and was God, the agent of creation, the life and the light, and the Word made flesh (1:14). It introduces the main themes of the Gospel: light and darkness, life, witness (John the Baptist), belief and rejection. Without it the reader of the signs and 'I am' sayings would not know that Jesus is the Word of God. It also sets John the Baptist as a witness who is not himself the light."),
        PQ("Discuss the significance of the raising of Lazarus in John 11. (Plan.)",
           "Point 1: a sign of Jesus' power over death for the glory of God (11:4). Point 2: shows the meaning of 'I am the resurrection and the life' and the response of Martha (11:25-27). Point 3: provokes the leaders to plan to kill Jesus (11:47-53), so it connects the sign to the Passion. Point 4: it points to Jesus' own resurrection, the first fruit of the eventual resurrection in Christian belief. Evaluate: Lazarus is raised to ordinary life and Jesus to a new kind of life; the other Gospels do not tell this story, which raises questions for historians (check your textbook)."),
        PQ("Compare John with the Synoptic Gospels in three ways. (Model answer.)",
           "Content: John has no parables, exorcisms or birth stories, and adds Nicodemus, the Samaritan woman and Lazarus. Chronology: John has several visits to Jerusalem and three Passovers, whereas the Synoptics have one visit; the Temple cleansing comes early in John. Style and theology: long discourses and symbols, the 'I am' sayings and an openly high Christology. Conclude that the differences show different selection and purpose, not that one Gospel is false."),
        TIP("Exam link: John is one of the six sections in the Board's Paper 3 (Section 2.2). The report notes that few candidates chose these questions and that many gave narration where explanation was needed: identity at the start of the ministry (the Baptist's denial that he is the Christ), light of the world and spiritual blindness, the significance of Lazarus, the personality and mission of Jesus, and the trials and crucifixion."),
        SUM("John, the Fourth Gospel: author traditionally John son of Zebedee; many scholars think of a Johannine community; date about AD 90-100", "Purpose: belief that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and life (20:31); audience Jews and Gentiles", "Differences from the Synoptics: no parables or exorcisms, long discourses, Jerusalem visits, Temple cleansing early", "Prologue: the Word, life, light, witness; the seven signs each point to Jesus' identity", "Seven 'I am' sayings with a picture; light and spiritual blindness in chapter 9; life and resurrection in chapter 11", "Explain meaning rather than retell"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Passion and resurrection
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M4, topic="The passion and resurrection narratives",
    title="The passion and resurrection narratives in the four Gospels",
    slug="rsl2-passion-and-resurrection",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The shared outline of the arrest, trials, crucifixion and resurrection accounts, each Gospel's emphases (Mark, Matthew, Luke, John), the Emmaus story, the ending of Mark, and the main historical and theological questions.",
    objectives=[
        "Outline the common sequence of the passion narrative and the resurrection accounts",
        "State the particular emphasis of each Gospel in the passion and resurrection",
        "Explain the trials of Jesus (before the Jewish authorities and before Pilate) and the issues scholars raise",
        "Explain the significance of the Emmaus story in Luke and the main theological meanings of the resurrection",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Passion narrative", "Last Supper", "Sanhedrin", "Pilate", "Crucifixion", "Empty tomb", "Appearances", "Emmaus", "Great Commission"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In a typical Gospel the last week of Jesus takes up a very large part of the book: about a third of Mark, and in John almost half. This shows how important the **passion** (the suffering and death of Jesus) and the **resurrection** were to the writers. In the Board's report both the trials and crucifixion (in John) and the Emmaus experience (in Luke) appear as questions, and the comments say that candidates often narrated events but did not explain what the events meant in each Gospel."),
        T("We approach the narratives in three steps: the common outline, the emphasis of each Gospel, and the questions scholars ask. As with other topics, we say what Christians believe and what historians discuss without deciding between them."),
        H("The common outline"),
        KEY("The passion in order", "All four Gospels follow roughly this sequence, with differences of detail.", cards=[
            ["Entry and the Last Supper", "Jesus enters Jerusalem, the Temple is cleansed (late in the Synoptics, early in John), and Jesus shares a final meal. In the Synoptics he gives bread and wine a new meaning; John instead has the washing of feet (John 13)."],
            ["Gethsemane and arrest", "Jesus prays in the garden, Judas arrives with an armed group, the disciples flee."],
            ["Before the Jewish authorities", "Jesus is taken to the high priest. In the Synoptics there is a hearing before the Sanhedrin (the Jewish council), in John a first appearance before Annas. Peter denies him."],
            ["Before Pilate", "The Roman governor Pontius Pilate questions Jesus about being 'king of the Jews'. Barabbas is released and Jesus is sentenced."],
            ["Crucifixion, death and burial", "Jesus is crucified at Golgotha with two others, dies, and is buried in a tomb by Joseph of Arimathea."],
            ["Resurrection", "Women find the tomb empty on the first day of the week; the risen Jesus appears to disciples."],
        ]),
        H("The emphasis of each Gospel"),
        T("**Mark** presents a Jesus who is deeply alone: the disciples flee, God seems silent and Jesus cries out in distress on the cross (15:34). Yet the temple curtain is torn and a Roman centurion calls him the Son of God (15:39). The shortest ancient ending (16:8) leaves the women afraid at the empty tomb. The longer ending (16:9-20) was added later, and most scholars say it is not original, which is why many Bibles print it in a different type or note it."),
        T("**Matthew** shows the passion as the fulfilment of scripture: many quotations from the prophets explain the events. Pilate washes his hands (27:24). There is an earthquake at the death of Jesus and again at the resurrection, and the guard at the tomb story answers the charge that the disciples stole the body (27:62-66; 28:11-15). The Gospel ends on a mountain in Galilee with the **Great Commission**: the risen Jesus tells his disciples to make disciples of all nations, baptising and teaching, and promises to be with them always (28:16-20)."),
        T("**Luke** stresses the innocence of Jesus: Pilate says three times that he finds no guilt in him (23:4, 14, 22) and the centurion says that this man was righteous or innocent (23:47). Jesus is pictured as compassionate even on the way to death: he speaks to the women of Jerusalem (23:27-31), and to one of the two criminals promises that he will be in paradise that day (23:43). Many manuscripts include his prayer for forgiveness for those who crucify him (23:34); some early ones omit it, so check your textbook. In the resurrection narrative Luke has the Emmaus story and ends with the ascension, with Acts continuing the story (Luke 24:50-53; Acts 1)."),
        T("**John** presents Jesus as in control of events: he goes to meet the arresting party (18:4-8), he speaks at length to Pilate about the nature of his kingship ('my kingdom is not of this world', 18:36), and his last word is 'It is finished' (19:30). John gives special trial scenes: first before Annas (18:12-24), then Caiaphas (18:24, 28), then Pilate in a series of scenes in and out of the governor's headquarters (18:28-19:16). The theme of kingship and truth runs through the trial before Pilate. In the resurrection narrative John has Mary Magdalene at the tomb, the disciples' race to the tomb, the appearance to Thomas ('My Lord and my God', 20:28) and the restoration of Peter at the lake (21:15-19)."),
        H("The Emmaus road (Luke 24:13-35)"),
        T("On the day of the resurrection two disciples, one named Cleopas, walk from Jerusalem to a village called Emmaus, about seven miles away. They are discussing the events and are downcast: they had hoped that Jesus would redeem Israel. A stranger joins them, explains the scriptures to them, showing that the Christ had to suffer and then enter his glory, and they do not recognise him. At table, when he takes bread, blesses and breaks it, their eyes are opened, and he disappears. They return at once to Jerusalem, where they find the eleven saying that the Lord has risen indeed and has appeared to Simon."),
        T("Note the meaning. **Emmaus is a village, not a person** (the Board's report notes that a few candidates took it as a human being). The story shows the risen Jesus as the one who explains the scriptures and is known in the breaking of bread. It moves from despair to joy and mission. It is a sign of hope and faith for later believers who did not see the risen Jesus, which is a key concern for Luke."),
        H("Questions that historians and scholars ask"),
        T("**Who was responsible for the death of Jesus?** The Gospels describe cooperation between some Jewish leaders and the Roman governor. Crucifixion was a Roman punishment. Scholars point out that Pilate had the legal power, and that it would be wrong to blame the Jewish people as a whole, an idea that has caused great harm in history and that responsible scholars reject. The Gospels give different details about the hearings, and scholars discuss how far the Gospel accounts reflect later tensions between the Church and the synagogue."),
        T("**What happened at the resurrection?** All four Gospels tell of the empty tomb and the appearances, but differ on details such as the number of women, the angels and the place of the appearances (Jerusalem in Luke and John, Galilee in Matthew and Mark's promise). Paul gives an earlier list of appearances in 1 Corinthians 15:3-8, which is usually studied in the Upper Sixth. Christians believe the resurrection was a real event in which God raised Jesus to a new life. Some scholars accept that the empty tomb and appearances are historical, some regard them as faith interpretations. A good answer sets out the evidence and the positions, and does not decide on behalf of the reader."),
        EX("Worked example 1: Luke's emphasis in the passion", intro="Question (written for practice): Describe the particular emphasis of Luke's passion narrative.", steps=[
            S("Innocence", text="Pilate three times finds no guilt (23:4, 14, 22); the centurion says that Jesus was righteous (23:47)."),
            S("Compassion and forgiveness", text="Jesus speaks to the women (23:27-31), promises paradise to the criminal who asks to be remembered (23:43), and (in many manuscripts) prays for forgiveness for those who crucify him (23:34)."),
            S("Calm trust", text="His last word is a prayer of trust: 'Father, into your hands I commit my spirit' (23:46)."),
            S("Link", text="Luke wants his reader to see a righteous sufferer who is faithful to God and who is vindicated by the resurrection and ascension."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: Emmaus", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain the significance of the Emmaus story in Luke.", steps=[
            S("Summary (brief)", text="Two disciples on the way to Emmaus meet the risen Jesus without recognising him, are taught from scripture, and recognise him in the breaking of bread."),
            S("Significance 1", text="The risen Christ is the fulfilment of scripture: the Messiah had to suffer and enter his glory."),
            S("Significance 2", text="The Lord is known in the breaking of bread, which connects later Christian meals to the presence of Jesus."),
            S("Significance 3", text="Despair turns to joy and mission: the disciples return to Jerusalem. It is a sign of hope and faith."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Confusing the four accounts of the trial and mixing details", "Learn what is special to each: the Sanhedrin hearing in Mark, Pilate's handwashing in Matthew, Pilate's three declarations of innocence in Luke, the trial scenes and 'my kingdom is not of this world' in John."),
        MISTAKE("Blaming 'the Jews' for the death of Jesus", "Responsible study treats the Gospels as showing that particular leaders and the Roman governor were involved. The Jewish people as a whole are not responsible, and examiners expect a careful, historical way of writing."),
        QUIZ("Which Gospel ends with the Great Commission on a mountain in Galilee?", ["Mark", "Matthew", "Luke", "John"], 1,
             "Matthew 28:16-20 gives the commission to make disciples of all nations.", hint="This Gospel often shows prophecy fulfilled.",
             feedback=["Mark's earliest ending is at the empty tomb with the women afraid.", "", "Luke ends with the ascension near Bethany and joy in the Temple.", "John ends with an appearance at the lake and the restoration of Peter."]),
        QUIZ("Emmaus is", ["a disciple of Jesus", "a village about seven miles from Jerusalem", "the name of the high priest", "a mountain in Galilee"], 1,
             "Luke 24:13 names it as a village.", hint="Think of the road where two disciples walk.",
             feedback=["No disciple is called Emmaus; one of the two is Cleopas.", "", "The high priest at the time of the trial was Caiaphas.", "The commission in Matthew is on a mountain, but that is not Emmaus."]),
        PQ("Explain the importance of the Emmaus experience in Luke's Gospel. (Model answer.)",
           "Emmaus is a village near Jerusalem. Two downcast disciples meet the risen Jesus, who explains from the scriptures that the Christ must suffer and enter his glory; they recognise him in the breaking of bread, and they return at once to Jerusalem. The story proves that Jesus has risen, that the cross fulfils scripture, and that the risen Lord is present for later believers in the scriptures and in the shared meal. It turns despair to hope and mission. Luke wants readers who never saw the risen Jesus to be confident of his presence."),
        PQ("Compare the passion narratives of Mark and John. (Model answer.)",
           "Mark: Jesus is alone and abandoned; he prays in distress in Gethsemane and cries out on the cross; the centurion's confession is the climax; the Gospel ends at the empty tomb with fear. John: Jesus is in control, goes to meet his arrest, speaks of his kingdom before Pilate and says 'It is finished'; the trials have special scenes (Annas, Pilate's dialogue); the resurrection includes Mary Magdalene, Thomas and the restoration of Peter. Conclude that Mark stresses suffering and hiddenness, John stresses glory and kingship; both agree on the main sequence."),
        PQ("Discuss the view that the Gospels differ so much on the resurrection that no historical conclusion can be drawn. (Plan.)",
           "For: differences on women, angels and places (Jerusalem or Galilee). Against: they agree on the empty tomb, women as first witnesses and appearances; Paul's early list in 1 Corinthians 15; differences are normal for independent accounts. Positions: Christians believe in a real event; some scholars see historical core, others faith language. Evaluate: conclusion depends partly on presuppositions about whether miracles are possible, so say that and conclude with a balanced judgement."),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's Paper 3 has questions on the trials and crucifixion (John, Section 2.2) and on Emmaus (Luke, Section 2.1). The report says candidates narrated events without bringing out the activity and identity of Jesus as the promised Saviour. In your answer say what each scene shows about Jesus."),
        SUM("The passion in all four Gospels: Last Supper, Gethsemane, hearings, Pilate, crucifixion, burial, empty tomb, appearances", "Mark: suffering and abandonment, the centurion's confession; Matthew: fulfilment and the Great Commission", "Luke: innocence, compassion, Emmaus and the ascension; John: Jesus in control, trial scenes, 'It is finished', Thomas", "Emmaus is a village; the story shows scripture fulfilled and the Lord known in the breaking of bread", "Historians discuss responsibility for the death of Jesus (Roman crucifixion) and the nature of the resurrection reports; write with care", "Explain what each scene means in its Gospel"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Spread and persecutions
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M5, topic="The spread of Christianity and the persecutions",
    title="The spread of Christianity in the Roman Empire and the persecutions",
    slug="rsl2-spread-and-persecutions",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Why Christianity spread in the Roman Empire (roads, language, synagogues, community life), why it was persecuted, the main persecutions from Nero to Diocletian, and the turn to toleration under Galerius and Constantine.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain the factors that helped the early Church to spread in the Roman Empire",
        "Explain the reasons for persecution and the difference between local and empire-wide persecution",
        "Describe the main persecutions (Nero, Trajan's policy, Decius, Diocletian) with dates",
        "Describe the end of persecution and the changed position of the Church under Constantine and later",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Pax Romana", "Diaspora", "Martyr", "Emperor worship", "Lapsi", "Edict of Toleration (311)", "Edict of Milan (313)", "Apostolic Church"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In Cameroon many churches began in small groups: a few families meeting in a house, then a congregation, then a network across the country. The first Christians were a similar movement. At the death of Jesus (around AD 30) there was a small group of followers in Palestine. Three centuries later Christianity was the favoured religion of the emperor. This lesson asks **how** this happened, and **what** the cost was."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("Why did Christianity spread?"),
        KEY("Factors that helped the spread", "Textbooks usually give a mix of political, social and religious reasons. Learn at least five.", cards=[
            ["Roman peace and roads", "The Pax Romana (a long period of relative peace from the time of Augustus) and the Roman roads and sea routes made travel safer and faster for missionaries such as Paul."],
            ["Common language", "Greek was widely understood across the eastern Mediterranean and Latin in the west. The New Testament was written in Greek."],
            ["Jewish synagogues and the Diaspora", "Jews lived in cities across the empire. Paul usually began in the synagogue, where he found Jews and also 'God-fearers' (Gentiles attracted to Judaism)."],
            ["The message and the community", "Christians offered forgiveness, hope of eternal life, equality in the Church of slaves and free, and care for the poor, widows, the sick and prisoners (even in times of plague)."],
            ["Witness of martyrs and missionaries", "Ordinary believers, merchants and soldiers carried the faith, and the courage of martyrs impressed observers."],
            ["Religious and social needs", "The older religions did not give personal assurance of salvation for many; mystery religions and philosophy answered some needs, but Christianity offered both belonging and moral teaching."],
        ]),
        T("The Acts of the Apostles (the Board treats Acts and 1 Corinthians as its own section) shows the first stage: Jerusalem, Judea and Samaria, and then outward to Antioch, Asia Minor, Greece and Rome. Other early Christian centres were **Alexandria** and **Carthage** in Africa. Africa has a great place in this story: the Christian writers Tertullian and Cyprian were in Carthage, and Origen and Athanasius were in Alexandria. Numbers are uncertain; scholars estimate and disagree, so do not give a statistic for Christians in AD 300 as if it were certain (check your textbook)."),
        H("Why were Christians persecuted?"),
        D("A **martyr** (from the Greek word for 'witness') is a person who dies rather than deny the faith. **Persecution** is the hostile treatment of a group because of its beliefs.", title="Definitions"),
        T("Rome was usually tolerant of local religions, provided that people also respected the state gods and the emperor. Christians were treated differently for several reasons. **Refusal to worship the gods and the emperor**: Christians refused to sacrifice to the emperor's spirit or to offer incense to the gods, and so were called 'atheists' and disloyal. **Secret and exclusive meetings**: rumours spread that Christians ate flesh and blood (a misunderstanding of the Lord's Supper) and behaved immorally in the 'love feasts'. **Social separation**: they avoided public festivals, the theatre and the arena, and trades connected with idols, so their neighbours saw them as strange and hostile. **Economic and political reasons**: local business interests, for example silversmiths of Ephesus in Acts 19, and the need for a scapegoat in difficult times (fire, plague, famine, war)."),
        H("The main persecutions"),
        IMG("alrsl2-persecutions-timeline.svg", alt="A timeline from AD 30 to AD 313 with the persecutions by Nero in 64, Trajan's letters about 112, Marcus Aurelius era in 177, Decius in 250, Valerian in 257, Diocletian from 303, the Edict of Toleration in 311 and the Edict of Milan in 313.", caption="Figure 1. Main events in the persecution of the early Church (dates as in many textbooks; check your textbook)."),
        T("**Nero (AD 64).** A great fire destroyed much of Rome. The Roman historian Tacitus reports that Nero blamed the Christians, who were arrested and killed with great cruelty. This persecution was local (in Rome) and not on the empire's own initiative. Christian tradition says that Peter and Paul died at Rome in the reign of Nero."),
        T("**Domitian (AD 81-96).** Many textbooks describe a persecution under Domitian, linked to the book of Revelation. Scholars debate its extent (check your textbook)."),
        T("**Trajan's policy (about AD 112).** Pliny, governor of Bithynia, wrote to Trajan to ask how to deal with Christians. Trajan replied that they were not to be hunted out, that anonymous accusations were to be ignored, but that those proved to be Christians and who refused to worship the Roman gods were to be punished. This shows that being a Christian could be punished in itself, and that persecution depended on local accusation."),
        T("**Second century.** Local persecutions occurred, for example the death of Polycarp, bishop of Smyrna, in the second century (the date is debated, around the mid-150s), and the martyrs of Lyons in about AD 177 in the time of Marcus Aurelius. The African martyrs Perpetua and Felicity died at Carthage in about AD 203."),
        T("**Decius (AD 249-251).** The first persecution planned for the whole empire. Decius ordered every citizen to offer sacrifice and receive a certificate (a libellus) as proof. Many Christians died, many fled, and many gave way. The question of what to do with **lapsi** (those who lapsed) divided the Church and led to disputes in places such as Carthage under Bishop Cyprian. The later controversy with the Donatists in North Africa grew from similar questions after the Great Persecution."),
        T("**Valerian (AD 257-258)** attacked Church leaders and property (Cyprian was martyred in 258). **Diocletian and the Great Persecution (from AD 303).** The most severe: Christian buildings and Scriptures were to be destroyed, clergy arrested, and later all were ordered to sacrifice. Some Christians handed over the Scriptures and were called **traditores**. The persecution varied from place to place and was less severe in the west after 305."),
        H("The end of persecution"),
        T("In AD 311 the emperor Galerius, who had been a leading persecutor, issued an **edict of toleration** that allowed Christians to practise their religion. In AD 313 Constantine and Licinius agreed a policy of religious toleration known as the **Edict of Milan** (some scholars prefer to call it a joint letter or agreement, check your textbook). Constantine favoured the Church with money and buildings, and he took part in its disputes, as the lesson on Nicaea shows. Under Theodosius I the Nicene form of Christianity was declared the religion of the empire in AD 380."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reasons for persecution", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain why Christians were persecuted in the Roman Empire.", steps=[
            S("Point 1", text="Refusal to worship the gods and the emperor made them appear disloyal and 'atheist'."),
            S("Point 2", text="Rumours about their meetings (cannibalism, immorality) came from misunderstanding the Lord's Supper and 'love feasts'."),
            S("Point 3", text="Separation from public life and the pagan festivals offended neighbours and trades."),
            S("Point 4", text="Emperors such as Nero used Christians as scapegoats; others (Decius, Diocletian) saw Christianity as a threat to unity and traditional religion."),
            S("Evaluate", text="Persecution was often local and depended on accusations, until Decius and Diocletian ordered it empire-wide."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: Trajan's reply to Pliny", intro="Question (written for practice): What does the exchange between Pliny and Trajan show about the legal position of Christians?", steps=[
            S("Situation", text="Pliny, a governor, was unsure how to treat people accused of being Christians."),
            S("Trajan's reply", text="Do not seek them out; do not accept anonymous charges; if they are brought forward and refuse to worship the gods, punish them; if they deny and worship, pardon them."),
            S("Meaning", text="The name 'Christian' was a punishable offence if the accused would not obey, but there was no policy of search. Persecution was therefore uneven."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying that Christians were persecuted continuously for 300 years", "Persecution was usually local and irregular, with long peaceful periods. Empire-wide persecutions came under Decius, Valerian and Diocletian. Give dates and say which were local and which were general."),
        MISTAKE("Stating exact numbers of Christians or martyrs", "The figures that appear in books are estimates and scholars disagree. Say 'many' or 'a growing number', and mention the debate if the question asks about growth."),
        QUIZ("Which emperor ordered the first empire-wide persecution, requiring all citizens to sacrifice?", ["Nero", "Trajan", "Decius", "Constantine"], 2,
             "Decius (AD 249-251) ordered every citizen to sacrifice and receive a certificate.", hint="Think of the certificates (libelli).",
             feedback=["Nero's persecution was local to Rome in AD 64.", "Trajan's policy was not to search for Christians.", "", "Constantine ended persecution."]),
        QUIZ("What is the Edict of Milan (313)?", ["The first order to persecute Christians", "The statement of the Nicene Creed", "A policy of toleration agreed by Constantine and Licinius", "The decision to make Christianity the only legal religion"], 2,
             "It established toleration; making Nicene Christianity the religion of the empire came later under Theodosius.", hint="Think of 313, not 380.",
             feedback=["It ended persecution, it did not start it.", "The Creed came from the Council of Nicaea in 325.", "", "That was the later work of Theodosius, around 380."]),
        PQ("Explain four reasons for the spread of Christianity in the first three centuries. (Model answer.)",
           "(1) The Pax Romana, Roman roads and sea routes allowed missionaries to travel (Paul's journeys). (2) The common language, Greek, enabled preaching and writing. (3) Jewish synagogues in the Diaspora gave a first audience, including God-fearers. (4) The Christian community offered forgiveness, hope, equality across social classes and care of the poor and sick. (5) The courage of martyrs made an impression on observers. Evaluate: these factors helped but do not by themselves explain the growth; Christians themselves point to the work of the Holy Spirit."),
        PQ("Describe the Decian and Diocletianic persecutions and their effect on the Church. (Model answer.)",
           "Decius (249-251) ordered all citizens to sacrifice and obtain a certificate; the first empire-wide persecution. Many died, many fled, many lapsed. The question of how to receive lapsed Christians back divided the Church. Diocletian (from 303) ordered churches destroyed, Scriptures burned, clergy imprisoned and later sacrifices demanded; those who handed over Scriptures were called traditores. The effects: courage of martyrs, disputes over lapsed and traitors (leading to schism such as Donatism in North Africa), and a stronger Church organisation; in 311 and 313 persecution was ended."),
        PQ("'Persecution helped Christianity to grow.' Discuss. (Plan.)",
           "For: the witness of martyrs impressed observers (Tertullian is often said to have argued this); Christians were united; faith was seen as worth dying for. Against: persecutions killed leaders and destroyed buildings; many lapsed and disputes followed; growth was also due to other factors (roads, language, community care); persecution was irregular. Evaluate: persecution was a test, not the main cause of growth. Conclude with a clear judgement."),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's report does not name Early Church History as a separate section, but the same themes appear in Acts questions (the persecution of the believers in Jerusalem that became a blessing to the growth of the Church; the Jerusalem Council). Check with your teacher which track your school teaches. Use the framework 'cause, example, effect'."),
        SUM("Factors in the spread: Pax Romana, roads, Greek, synagogues, the Christian community, martyrs", "Reasons for persecution: refusal to worship the emperor and gods, misunderstanding of meetings, separation, scapegoating", "Main events: Nero 64, Pliny and Trajan about 112, Decius 249-251, Valerian 257-258, Diocletian from 303", "Many persecutions were local; Decius and Diocletian were empire-wide", "Toleration: Galerius 311, Milan 313; Nicene Christianity the state religion under Theodosius, 380", "Do not invent numbers; mark disputed dates"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Apologists and heresies
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M5, topic="Apologists and heresies",
    title="Apologists and early heresies: Gnosticism, Marcionism and Montanism",
    slug="rsl2-apologists-and-heresies",
    minutes=24,
    summary="How second- and third-century Christian writers defended the faith (the apologists) and how the Church responded to Gnosticism, Marcionism and Montanism by developing a rule of faith, a canon of Scripture and church order.",
    objectives=[
        "Define apology, apologist, orthodoxy and heresy and explain why the terms are debated",
        "Describe the aims and main arguments of the apologists (Justin Martyr, Tertullian, Origen)",
        "Summarise the beliefs of Gnosticism, Marcionism and Montanism and the Church's response to each",
        "Explain how the disputes helped to shape the canon, the rule of faith and church order",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Apology", "Heresy and orthodoxy", "Gnosticism", "Docetism", "Marcion", "Canon", "Rule of faith", "Montanism", "Apostolic succession"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A student who stands up in a debate must answer two kinds of challenge: those who attack the whole idea (outsiders) and those who misrepresent it from inside (members who teach something different). The early Church faced both. Educated writers called **apologists** answered the outsiders. Church leaders answered those they called **heretics** within."),
        T("Remember that this lesson describes what each group taught and how the Church responded. We use the words 'orthodox' and 'heresy' as the Church used them, but note that some historians argue that the early Church was more varied than the later picture suggests (a thesis associated with Walter Bauer, which is debated). Keep your description fair."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("Apologists: defending the faith"),
        D("An **apology** (from the Greek apologia, 'a defence') is a reasoned defence of the Christian faith, normally addressed to non-Christians, officials or an emperor. An **apologist** is a writer of such a defence.", title="Definitions"),
        T("The apologists wrote in the second and third centuries. Their aims were: to **answer false charges** (atheism, immorality, disloyalty); to show that Christians were **good citizens** who prayed for the emperor and paid taxes; to argue that Christianity is **reasonable** and the 'true philosophy'; and to criticise idol worship. A main idea in many was that the **Logos** (the Word or Reason of God, as in John 1) is found in part in the best of Greek philosophy and fully in Christ."),
        KEY("Three apologists to know", "Check your textbook for the writers your course names.", cards=[
            ["Justin Martyr (died about 165)", "From Palestine, taught in Rome and was executed there. His Apologies argue that Christians are innocent of the charges and that the Logos explains the truth found in philosophy. His Dialogue with Trypho debates with a Jew."],
            ["Tertullian (about 160-220)", "From Carthage in North Africa, the first great Christian writer in Latin. His Apology defends Christians to Roman officials. He is known for his forceful style, his teaching about the Trinity and his later move to the Montanists."],
            ["Origen (about 185-253)", "Of Alexandria, a scholar of Scripture and philosophy. His work against the pagan critic Celsus replies to detailed arguments. He taught that Scripture has several levels of meaning."],
        ]),
        T("Irenaeus, bishop of Lyons (late second century), is better known for his work against heresies than as an apologist. Others include Aristides, Athenagoras and Clement of Alexandria. African writers such as Tertullian and, later, Cyprian show how important Africa was to the development of Christian thought."),
        H("Orthodoxy and heresy"),
        D("**Orthodoxy** means 'right belief'; **heresy** (from a Greek word meaning 'choice') was the word used by the Church for teaching it judged to be a wrong choice that departed from the apostolic faith.", title="Definitions"),
        T("How did the Church decide what was right? It used three tests that developed from the second century. First, the **rule of faith**, a summary of the apostolic teaching handed on at baptism, an early form of what became the creeds. Second, **Scripture**, and the gradual agreement on which books belong to the New Testament (the **canon**). Third, **apostolic succession**: the argument, strongly used by Irenaeus, that the teaching of the apostles was handed on in churches founded by them through a line of bishops."),
        H("Gnosticism"),
        T("**Gnosticism** was not one single church but a number of related movements, active especially in the second century, with teachers such as Valentinus and Basilides in Egypt and Rome. The name comes from **gnosis**, the Greek word for knowledge: salvation came through secret knowledge revealed to the few. Common features, though they varied among groups: the material world is evil or flawed, created by a lesser being (the **demiurge**) and not by the supreme God; the human spirit is a spark from the true God trapped in the body; the Saviour brings the knowledge that frees it; and the Saviour is a heavenly being who only seemed to have a human body. The last idea is called **docetism** (from the Greek 'to seem')."),
        T("Gnostic writings were largely known from the Church's critics until 1945, when a large collection of Gnostic and related texts was found at **Nag Hammadi** in Egypt. These include the Gospel of Thomas and the Gospel of Truth. Scholars use them to see Gnostic teaching in its own words."),
        T("**Church response.** Irenaeus wrote against the Gnostics (the work is known as Against Heresies) and argued that there is one God, who is both creator and father of Jesus; that Christ was truly human; that salvation includes the body; and that the faith is the public teaching of the apostolic churches, not a secret tradition. The Church kept the Old Testament, wrote creeds that affirmed God as creator, and moved toward a definite list of New Testament books."),
        H("Marcionism"),
        T("**Marcion** (from Sinope on the Black Sea) came to Rome and was excluded from the Church there in about AD 144. He taught that the God of the Old Testament, a just and angry creator, was different from the loving Father revealed by Jesus. He rejected the Old Testament and produced the first known list of Christian Scriptures: an edited Gospel of Luke and ten letters of Paul. He founded churches that lasted for some time."),
        T("**Church response.** Church leaders (Irenaeus, Tertullian) rejected the separation of the two Gods and defended the unity of the Bible and of God. The challenge pushed the Church to state its own canon more clearly: it kept the Old Testament and accepted four Gospels and a larger group of apostolic writings, not the edited Luke and Paul of Marcion. Marcion is therefore important in the history of the canon."),
        H("Montanism"),
        T("**Montanism** (also called the New Prophecy) began in Phrygia in Asia Minor in the later second century (dates vary, about AD 170 in many books). Montanus and two women prophets, Priscilla and Maximilla, claimed that the Holy Spirit spoke through them in ecstatic prophecy. They taught that a new age of the Spirit had begun, expected the heavenly Jerusalem to come down at the village of Pepuza, and demanded strict moral discipline (fasting, refusal to flee persecution, no second marriage). Tertullian later joined the movement."),
        T("**Church response.** Leaders did not oppose the idea that the Spirit still works. They opposed the claim that new revelation could stand beside or above the apostolic teaching, the ecstatic form of the prophecy, and the harsh discipline. The Church affirmed that revelation was complete in Christ and the apostles and gave authority to bishops and councils."),
        KEY("Comparison", "A summary for revision (details vary by textbook).", cards=[
            ["Gnosticism", "Secret knowledge; evil matter; a lesser creator; Christ only seemed human. Response: Irenaeus, rule of faith, apostolic succession, creeds."],
            ["Marcionism", "Two Gods; rejection of the Old Testament; an edited Luke and ten Pauline letters. Response: unity of God and Bible, definite canon."],
            ["Montanism", "New prophecy of the Spirit; strict discipline; expectation of the end. Response: revelation complete in the apostles, church authority, mixture of discipline and order."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: why were the apologists writing?", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain the aims of the second-century apologists.", steps=[
            S("Aim 1", text="To answer the charges against Christians (atheism, immorality, disloyalty)."),
            S("Aim 2", text="To show Christians were good citizens who respected the law and prayed for rulers."),
            S("Aim 3", text="To argue that Christianity was the true philosophy, with the Logos as the link (Justin)."),
            S("Example", text="Justin in Rome, Tertullian in Carthage, Origen against Celsus."),
            S("Evaluation", text="The apologists could be misunderstood by the Church: some felt that they gave too much to philosophy. Their writings also helped later theology."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: Marcion and the canon", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain how Marcion influenced the formation of the New Testament canon.", steps=[
            S("Marcion's list", text="He rejected the Old Testament and accepted only an edited Luke and ten Pauline letters."),
            S("Reaction", text="The Church had to say clearly what its Scriptures were: the Old Testament, and the four Gospels with the letters of Paul and other apostolic writings."),
            S("Evaluation", text="Many scholars say the Church was already using these books and Marcion's list only made the question urgent. The canon was settled over a longer period, not at one moment."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Describing Gnostics as one organised church with the same beliefs", "Gnosticism covers many groups with different teachers and teachings. Say 'some Gnostic groups taught' and use the common features carefully."),
        MISTAKE("Using 'heresy' as an insult in your own voice", "In a Religious Studies essay say 'the Church judged this teaching to be heresy' and explain the reasons. Do not write as if you were taking one side in the argument."),
        QUIZ("Which apologist wrote in Latin and lived in Carthage?", ["Justin Martyr", "Origen", "Tertullian", "Athanasius"], 2,
             "Tertullian of Carthage (about 160-220) was the first great Latin Christian writer.", hint="Think of North Africa.",
             feedback=["Justin taught in Rome and wrote in Greek.", "Origen worked in Alexandria and Caesarea, writing in Greek.", "", "Athanasius was bishop of Alexandria in the fourth century and is linked to Nicaea."]),
        QUIZ("What was Marcion's main teaching?", ["The Old Testament God was different from the Father of Jesus", "Matter is good because God made it", "The Spirit ended all prophecy", "Christ was an angel created by the Father"], 0,
             "He separated the just creator of the Old Testament from the loving Father of Jesus.", hint="Think of two Gods.",
             feedback=["", "That would be a view of the Church against Gnosticism.", "Montanus claimed the opposite: that the Spirit was prophesying.", "That describes the later view of Arius (next lesson), not Marcion."]),
        PQ("Describe the main beliefs of the Gnostics and how the Church responded. (Model answer.)",
           "Gnostics taught salvation through secret knowledge (gnosis); matter was evil or flawed, made by a lesser creator (the demiurge); the divine spark in humans was to be freed; the Saviour came to give knowledge and only seemed to be human (docetism). Not all groups agreed. Church response: Irenaeus's Against Heresies; the one God is creator and Father; Christ was truly human; salvation includes the body; the faith is public teaching of the apostolic churches (apostolic succession); and the rule of faith, canon and creeds were firmed up. The Nag Hammadi texts (1945) allow scholars to read Gnostic writings directly."),
        PQ("Compare Marcionism and Montanism. (Model answer.)",
           "Marcion: a theological dispute about God and Scripture (two Gods, a rejected Old Testament, a shortened New Testament). Montanus: a dispute about the Spirit and authority (new prophecy, ecstatic speech, severe discipline, expectation of the end). Both arose in the second century in Asia Minor or from Asia Minor and both challenged church authority; the Church responded to the first by clarifying the canon and the unity of God, and to the second by saying revelation was complete in the apostles and by asserting the authority of bishops. Both show that the Church needed clear tests of teaching."),
        PQ("'The heresies were good for the Church.' Discuss. (Plan.)",
           "For: they forced the Church to clarify its faith, define the canon, develop creeds and defend the unity of Scripture; Irenaeus and Tertullian made lasting contributions. Against: they caused division and suffering, and some groups were treated harshly; some scholars say the Church's answers closed down the variety that existed at first. Evaluate: answer depends on whether you take the view that there was a clear 'orthodoxy' from the beginning (the traditional view) or that the early Church was more diverse (Bauer). Conclude with a balanced judgement."),
        TIP("Exam link: expect structured questions that ask you to describe a heresy and the Church's response, or to explain the aims of an apologist. Check with your teacher whether your track includes this, since the Board's 2023 report does not name Early Church History as a section. The skill (describe, explain cause and response) is the same as in the Acts questions that the report describes."),
        SUM("Apologists (Justin Martyr, Tertullian, Origen) defended Christianity, showed that Christians were loyal and reasonable, and used the Logos idea", "Church tests of right teaching: rule of faith, canon of Scripture, apostolic succession", "Gnosticism: gnosis, evil matter, a lesser creator, docetism; Irenaeus replied; Nag Hammadi texts found in 1945", "Marcion: two Gods, an edited Luke and ten Pauline letters; helped to prompt a clearer canon", "Montanism: new prophecy, strict discipline; the Church held that revelation was complete in the apostles", "Describe fairly: 'the Church judged this to be heresy'"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Nicaea and Chalcedon
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M5, topic="The Councils of Nicaea and Chalcedon",
    title="The Arian controversy and the Councils of Nicaea (325) and Chalcedon (451)",
    slug="rsl2-nicaea-chalcedon",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The dispute over the Son's relation to the Father (Arius and Athanasius), what the Council of Nicaea decided and why, the later Christological disputes, the Definition of Chalcedon, and the divisions that followed.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain the teaching of Arius and the response of Alexander and Athanasius",
        "Describe the Council of Nicaea: who called it, what it decided and its limits",
        "Explain the Christological disputes after Nicaea and the Chalcedonian Definition",
        "Evaluate the importance and the consequences of the councils, including the churches that did not accept Chalcedon",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Arianism", "Homoousios", "Nicene Creed", "Trinity", "Nestorius", "Theotokos", "Two natures in one person", "Chalcedonian Definition"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Every Sunday millions of Christians in Cameroon and across the world say a creed together. The words of the creed they use in many churches were agreed in the fourth century in disputes about one question: who is Jesus Christ, and how does he stand in relation to God? The Councils of Nicaea (325) and Chalcedon (451) are the two most famous steps in that story."),
        T("The word **council** here means a gathering of bishops to decide on a question of teaching or order. Some councils were called 'ecumenical' (from a Greek word for 'the whole inhabited world'), meaning that they were intended for the whole Church. The Roman emperor, after the Edict of Milan, helped to summon them."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("The Arian controversy"),
        T("Around AD 318 a disagreement arose in Alexandria, Egypt. **Arius**, a presbyter (priest) of the city, taught that the Son of God was the first and greatest of God's creatures, made before time but still created, so that there was a time when the Son did not exist. Only the Father, he said, is truly without beginning. The Son is therefore like God but not equal to God and not of the same being as God. His bishop, **Alexander**, taught that the Son shares in the eternal being of the Father. The dispute became bitter and spread through the eastern part of the empire."),
        D("**Arianism**: the teaching associated with Arius that the Son is a creature, made by the Father, subordinate to him and not eternal in the same sense as the Father.", title="Arianism"),
        T("Why did it matter? For the Church, the question was about **salvation** and **worship**. If the Son is a creature, can he truly bring humans to God and can Christians worship him as God without worshipping a creature? **Athanasius**, at first a deacon with Alexander and later bishop of Alexandria, argued that only if the Son is truly God can he save humanity. The opponents of Arius appealed to Scripture, for example John 1:1, while Arius's supporters appealed to texts that seem to speak of the Son as subordinate (for example John 14:28 and Proverbs 8:22, which they read as speaking of the Son)."),
        H("The Council of Nicaea (AD 325)"),
        T("The emperor **Constantine**, who wanted unity in the empire he had just reunited, called a council of bishops to meet at **Nicaea** (modern Iznik in Turkey). Tradition numbers the bishops at 318, but scholars estimate that about 250 to 300 attended, most of them from the East. Constantine himself took part in the opening. The main decisions were:"),
        KEY("What Nicaea decided", "Learn these points with the date.", cards=[
            ["The creed", "The council produced a creed that said the Son is begotten and not made, and 'of one substance' (Greek homoousios) with the Father. The word was not in Scripture but was chosen to exclude the Arian view."],
            ["Condemnation of Arius", "Arius and those who held that the Son was a creature were condemned and Arius was exiled."],
            ["The date of Easter", "The council took steps toward a common date for Easter."],
            ["Other canons", "It also issued rules on church order (such as the places of the bishops of Alexandria, Antioch and Rome and the treatment of those who had lapsed)."],
        ]),
        T("Nicaea did not end the dispute. For some fifty years the argument continued: supporters of a middle position (the Son is 'of like substance', homoiousios) and of Arius, as well as Athanasius, who was exiled several times, struggled for influence, and some emperors favoured the Arian side. Arian forms of Christianity also spread among some Germanic peoples. The Council of **Constantinople (AD 381)** reaffirmed Nicaea and enlarged the creed to speak of the Holy Spirit. The creed used in many churches today is commonly called the Nicene Creed, but in the form used it comes from Constantinople (check your textbook)."),
        IMG("alrsl2-councils-timeline.svg", alt="Timeline of four councils: Nicaea in 325 on the Son and the Father, Constantinople in 381 on the Holy Spirit and the creed, Ephesus in 431 on Nestorius and Mary as Theotokos, Chalcedon in 451 on two natures in one person.", caption="Figure 1. The first four ecumenical councils and the main question of each."),
        H("From Nicaea to Chalcedon"),
        T("Once it was agreed that Christ is fully God, a second question came up: how are the divine and the human united in the one Christ? Two schools of thought are often contrasted. **Alexandria** stressed the unity of the person of Christ (the Word became flesh). **Antioch** stressed the full reality of his humanity and the difference between the two natures. Disagreement came to a head under **Nestorius**, patriarch of Constantinople, who was understood to object to calling Mary **Theotokos** ('God-bearer', mother of God) because it seemed to confuse the human and divine. Cyril of Alexandria opposed him, and the Council of **Ephesus (AD 431)** condemned Nestorius's teaching as dividing Christ into two persons. Scholars still discuss whether Nestorius really taught what was condemned."),
        T("On the other side, **Eutyches**, a monk in Constantinople, taught that after the union the human nature was absorbed into the divine, so that Christ had one nature (a teaching often called Monophysitism, from the Greek for 'one nature'). Pope **Leo I** of Rome wrote a letter (the Tome) which stated that Christ has two natures in one person. The emperor Marcian called a council at **Chalcedon** (near Constantinople) in AD 451."),
        D("**The Definition of Chalcedon (AD 451)** teaches that Jesus Christ is one person in two natures, fully God and fully human, and that the two natures are united 'without confusion, without change, without division, without separation'. It also affirmed the title Theotokos for Mary.", title="Chalcedonian Definition"),
        T("Chalcedon is viewed in the West and in the Eastern Orthodox Church as a model statement of the faith. However, it was rejected by many in Egypt, Syria and Armenia, who felt it came too close to Nestorius and who were also concerned about the power of Constantinople and the emperor. The churches that did not accept it are today called **Oriental Orthodox**: the Coptic Orthodox Church, the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, the Syriac Orthodox Church and the Armenian Apostolic Church, among others. Many modern discussions between these churches and the others conclude that the dispute was partly about words, but check your textbook for how your course treats this."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the main issue at Nicaea", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain the issue at the Council of Nicaea and the decision reached.", steps=[
            S("The question", text="Is the Son of God a creature, or is he of the same being as the Father?"),
            S("Arius", text="The Son was made by the Father, and there was a time when he was not; he is subordinate."),
            S("Alexander and Athanasius", text="The Son is eternal, truly God; only God can save."),
            S("The decision", text="Nicaea affirmed that the Son is begotten, not made, 'of one substance' with the Father; Arius was condemned."),
            S("Evaluation", text="The decision did not end the controversy; it took until 381 for Nicaea's teaching to become secure."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: why was Chalcedon needed?", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain the disputes that led to the Council of Chalcedon.", steps=[
            S("After Nicaea", text="Christ is fully God. The question: how are the divine and human joined?"),
            S("Two extremes", text="Nestorius (accused of dividing Christ into two persons) was condemned at Ephesus 431; Eutyches (accused of merging the natures into one) was opposed by Leo."),
            S("Definition", text="One person in two natures, without confusion, change, division or separation."),
            S("Result", text="The settlement of the main churches of the West and East, but the Oriental Orthodox churches did not accept it."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying that the Council of Nicaea wrote the whole creed that churches use today", "Nicaea produced the first form and the key word homoousios. The creed in common use was formed at Constantinople (381). Say 'the Nicene Creed in its usual form comes from Nicaea and Constantinople'."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the two questions", "Nicaea: how does the Son relate to the Father (Arius)? Chalcedon: how are the divine and human related in the one Christ? Keep the questions separate and say that the second grew from the first."),
        QUIZ("What was the main teaching of Arius?", ["The Son is equal to the Father in every way", "The Son is a creature made by the Father and not eternal in the same sense", "Christ had only one nature", "Mary should not be called Theotokos"], 1,
             "Arius taught that the Son was created, so that there was a time when he did not exist.", hint="It was about the Son and the Father, before Nestorius.",
             feedback=["That was the view of Alexander and Athanasius.", "", "One nature was the teaching attributed to Eutyches.", "That was the objection of Nestorius."]),
        QUIZ("What does the Definition of Chalcedon teach?", ["Christ is one person in two natures, fully God and fully human", "Christ is only divine and has no human nature", "The Son and the Father are different persons of different substance", "The Holy Spirit is a creature"], 0,
             "That is the standard summary of the Definition of 451.", hint="One person, two natures.",
             feedback=["", "That is closer to Eutyches's view.", "That is Arian.", "That view was condemned at Constantinople in 381."]),
        PQ("Explain the causes and results of the Arian controversy. (Model answer.)",
           "Cause: Arius of Alexandria taught that the Son was created and subordinate to the Father. His bishop Alexander and Athanasius rejected this as destroying salvation and worship. The dispute spread and threatened the unity of the empire, so Constantine called the Council of Nicaea (325). Results: the creed with homoousios, the condemnation of Arius, a common approach to the date of Easter; but the controversy continued for decades, and the settlement was confirmed at Constantinople in 381. Arian Christianity continued among some peoples for some time."),
        PQ("Describe the Council of Chalcedon and its effects. (Model answer.)",
           "Summoned by Marcian in 451 after disputes between Nestorius (condemned at Ephesus in 431) and Eutyches. Leo's Tome and the Definition: one person in two natures, without confusion, change, division or separation. Effects: it became the standard of faith for the Western and Eastern Orthodox churches; it was rejected by many Christians in Egypt, Syria and Armenia, who formed the Oriental Orthodox churches (Coptic, Ethiopian, Syriac and Armenian), which continue today. Evaluate: some modern scholars say the dispute was partly about words and politics."),
        PQ("'Constantine's involvement helped the Church.' Discuss with reference to Nicaea. (Plan.)",
           "For: he gave the Church protection and ended persecution, called the council and gave a place to meet and a means of bringing the bishops together; unity was achieved on the central question. Against: the emperor's involvement made the Church dependent on imperial favour; later emperors used the power to favour one side (Arian emperors); doctrinal disputes became political. Evaluate: the council was shaped by bishops' arguments, not by the emperor alone. Conclude with a balanced judgement."),
        TIP("Exam link: expect structured questions such as 'State two beliefs of Arius' or 'Explain the importance of Nicaea'. Give names, dates and the key terms (homoousios, Theotokos, two natures). The Board's report does not name Early Church History as a separate section, so check which track your school teaches; the method (state, explain, evaluate) is the same as in the Philosophy of Religion questions on God and Christ."),
        SUM("Arius: the Son is a creature; Alexander and Athanasius: the Son is truly God and can save", "Nicaea (325): called by Constantine; the creed with homoousios; Arius condemned; the dispute went on until Constantinople (381)", "Later disputes: Nestorius (Ephesus 431), Eutyches (one nature); Leo's Tome", "Chalcedon (451): one person in two natures, without confusion, change, division or separation", "Oriental Orthodox churches (Coptic, Ethiopian, Syriac, Armenian) did not accept Chalcedon", "Keep the two questions apart: Son and Father, then divine and human in Christ"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Arguments for the existence of God
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M5, topic="Arguments for the existence of God",
    title="Arguments for the existence of God: ontological, cosmological, teleological and moral",
    slug="rsl2-arguments-for-god",
    minutes=26,
    summary="The four classical arguments (Anselm and Descartes, Aquinas's Five Ways, Paley and Hume, Kant's moral argument): how each is built, the main objections and replies, and how to evaluate them in an essay.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish a priori and a posteriori arguments, and deductive and inductive arguments",
        "State the ontological, cosmological, teleological and moral arguments accurately",
        "Give the main objections to each argument (Gaunilo, Kant, Hume, Russell, Darwin) and the replies",
        "Evaluate an argument fairly, avoiding the claim that any of them 'proves' or 'disproves' God",
    ],
    key_concepts=["A priori and a posteriori", "Ontological argument", "Cosmological argument", "Contingent and necessary being", "Teleological argument", "Moral argument", "Five Ways", "Fallacy of composition"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In the compound where you live, you may have heard someone say 'something must have made this world'. A thoughtful friend may answer 'then who made God?' You have just heard the start of a philosophical argument. Philosophy of religion asks what reasons, if any, can be given for belief in God. The Board's report lists Philosophy of Religion as a section of Paper 3 and says that a question on the Five Ways (the *Quinque Viae*) was well known but often misunderstood: students had to explain the arguments from motion, from efficient causes and from contingency."),
        T("This lesson is for the Philosophy of Religion option of the alternative track. We set out four arguments, each with its objections, without saying which side is right. A good answer to 'Does the argument work?' is a reasoned judgement, not a statement of the writer's own religion or lack of it."),
        T(TRACK),
        H("Tools of argument"),
        D("An **a priori** argument is based on reasoning alone, without observing the world. An **a posteriori** argument starts from an observed fact about the world (for example, that things move or that the world is ordered). A **deductive** argument claims that if the premises are true the conclusion must be true; an **inductive** argument says that the conclusion is probable.", title="Definitions"),
        IMG("alrsl2-arguments-map.svg", alt="A chart of four arguments for God with their starting points: ontological from the idea of God, cosmological from the existence of the world, teleological from order and design, moral from conscience and moral duty.", caption="Figure 1. The four classical arguments: starting point and type."),
        H("The ontological argument"),
        T("The argument was formulated by **Anselm** of Canterbury (eleventh century) in his work Proslogion. It begins from a definition of God as the greatest being that can be conceived (a being such that nothing greater can be thought). Anselm argues: even someone who denies God understands this idea, so God exists at least in the mind. But something that exists both in the mind and in reality is greater than something that exists only in the mind. If God existed only in the mind, we could conceive of something greater (a God that exists in reality), which contradicts the definition. So God exists in reality. In a second form, Anselm argues that God has **necessary existence**: a being that could not fail to exist."),
        T("**Descartes** (seventeenth century) gave a version: God is the supreme perfect being; existence is a perfection; so a supremely perfect being must exist, just as a triangle must have three angles."),
        KEY("Objections and replies", "Learn the name, the point and one reply for each.", cards=[
            ["Gaunilo (Anselm's contemporary)", "The same pattern would prove that a 'perfect island' exists. Anselm's reply (and many later philosophers): the island has no maximum, whereas God is by definition the greatest conceivable being, so the parallel fails. Critics say the objection still shows the argument can be misused."],
            ["Kant", "Existence is not a real predicate: saying a thing exists does not add a property to the concept. A hundred real coins have no more coins in the concept than a hundred possible ones. So existence cannot be part of what makes God 'greater'. Reply: necessary existence is different from ordinary existence (Malcolm; Plantinga's modal version)."],
            ["General", "The argument defines God into existence; moving from an idea to reality needs more than a definition."],
        ]),
        H("The cosmological argument"),
        T("These arguments start with the fact that the world exists and argue to a first cause or necessary being. **Thomas Aquinas** (thirteenth century) offered five arguments, called the **Five Ways**. The first three are cosmological. **First way: motion**: whatever moves is moved by another; there cannot be an endless chain of movers, so there must be a first unmoved mover, which people call God. **Second way: efficient causes**: nothing causes itself; so there must be a first uncaused cause. **Third way: contingency**: things come into being and pass away (they are contingent); if everything were contingent, at some time nothing would have existed and nothing could now exist; so there must be a necessary being that does not depend on another. In the Board's wording, these are the arguments from motion, from active efficient causes and from contingency."),
        T("Aquinas argued that a chain of causes in which each member depends on the one before it (a series of causes that act now, as a hand moves a stick that moves a stone) cannot go back forever, because without a first member nothing would move at all. Later versions: **Leibniz** used the **principle of sufficient reason** (everything has a reason for its existence), and the **kalam** argument (developed in Islamic thought by al-Ghazali and defended in modern times by William Lane Craig) says the universe began and so needs a cause."),
        KEY("Objections and replies", "Cosmological argument.", cards=[
            ["Hume", "We have no experience of how universes begin, so we cannot say what a cause of the whole must be like. Also, we can conceive of any event without a cause, so cause is not a logical necessity."],
            ["Russell (in debate with Copleston)", "The fact that every member of a series has a cause does not mean the whole series has a cause (the fallacy of composition: every human has a mother does not mean humanity has a mother). The universe may simply be a 'brute fact'."],
            ["Infinite regress", "Perhaps there could be an endless series of causes. Reply: an endless series of dependent things would still have no explanation (Leibniz, Copleston)."],
            ["Who made God?", "Reply: the argument is about a being that is necessary or uncaused by definition, not an exception to a rule that everything has a cause."],
        ]),
        H("The teleological argument"),
        T("'Telos' is Greek for purpose or end. The argument starts from order, regularity and apparent purpose in the world. The fifth of Aquinas's Ways reasons that natural things without intelligence act toward ends, so they must be directed by an intelligent being. **William Paley** (1802) used the image of a watch: finding a watch on the ground, we conclude that it was made by a designer, because its parts work together for a purpose. The parts of living things, such as the eye, work together even more clearly, so there must be a designer of nature."),
        KEY("Objections and replies", "Teleological argument.", cards=[
            ["Hume", "The analogy between the world and a machine is weak: the world is more like an animal or plant than a watch; we have seen no other worlds being made; the argument at best shows a designer, not a single, all-powerful and good God; the world contains disorder and suffering. Reply: the order that exists is still striking."],
            ["Darwin and evolution", "Natural selection explains how complex living things can arise without a designer. Reply: believers say evolution may be the way God works, and some point to the fine-tuning of physical laws for life (a more modern form). Critics say fine-tuning arguments need care about how likely different values really are."],
            ["Mill and the problem of evil", "Nature shows waste and suffering, which do not fit the idea of a good designer."],
        ]),
        H("The moral argument"),
        T("**Kant** did not think theoretical arguments could prove God, but argued in his work on practical reason that belief in God is needed for morality. Moral duty is real. We are required to seek the highest good, in which virtue and happiness go together. But in this life the virtuous often suffer, so the highest good requires a being who can bring the two together: God (and an afterlife). God is therefore a **postulate** of practical reason. The Board's report notes that candidates were asked to discuss the view of God that Kant puts forward, and found it difficult; the key is that Kant presents God as a necessary assumption of moral life, not as a conclusion of theoretical proof."),
        T("Other versions: **Newman** argued from conscience, which seems to speak with authority, to a lawgiver; **C. S. Lewis** argued from the widespread sense of right and wrong to an objective moral law. Objections: **Freud** and others say conscience comes from upbringing; evolution can explain moral feelings; the **Euthyphro dilemma** (is something good because God commands it, or does God command it because it is good?) suggests that goodness might not depend on God; many philosophers hold that morality can be objective without God."),
        EX("Worked example 1: Aquinas's argument from motion", intro="Question (written for practice): Outline the argument from motion and one objection.", steps=[
            S("Observation", text="Some things in the world are in motion (change); whatever changes is changed by something else."),
            S("Regress", text="If each mover were itself moved by another without end, there would be no first mover, and so no movement now."),
            S("Conclusion", text="So there must be a first unmoved mover, which Aquinas identifies with God."),
            S("Objection", text="Hume and Russell: the argument does not show that the chain cannot be endless or that the first mover is the God of religion; modern physics speaks of motion differently from Aristotle."),
            S("Reply", text="Aquinas has in mind dependence at one moment, not a series in the past, and the question is about what keeps things in being."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: Kant's objection to the ontological argument", intro="Question (written for practice): Explain Kant's criticism.", steps=[
            S("Target", text="The step that says 'existing in reality is greater than existing in the mind'."),
            S("Claim", text="Existence is not a property that adds to a concept: the concept of 100 coins is the same whether or not they exist."),
            S("Result", text="So the idea of a perfect being does not entail its existence."),
            S("Reply", text="Defenders say Kant's point works for ordinary things, but not for a being whose nature is to be necessary."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying that an argument 'proves' or 'disproves' God", "Philosophers present these as arguments that may or may not be persuasive. Say 'the argument aims to show' and then weigh its strengths and weaknesses. Most religious believers do not rest their faith on these arguments alone."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing the four arguments", "Check each one: ontological (idea of God), cosmological (existence of the world or things), teleological (order and design), moral (conscience and duty). When asked for one, do not drift into another."),
        QUIZ("Which of Aquinas's Ways argues from things that come into being and pass away?", ["Argument from motion", "Argument from efficient causes", "Argument from contingency", "Argument from design"], 2,
             "The third way (contingency) argues from things that need not exist to a necessary being.", hint="Which word means 'might not have existed'?",
             feedback=["Motion argues from things being moved by another.", "Efficient causes argues from a chain of causes.", "", "Design is the fifth way."]),
        QUIZ("Which objection says that the 'perfect island' shows the ontological argument does not work?", ["Hume", "Gaunilo", "Paley", "Newman"], 1,
             "Gaunilo, a monk and contemporary of Anselm, argued this.", hint="He wrote 'on behalf of the fool'.",
             feedback=["Hume attacked the design and cosmological arguments.", "", "Paley defended the argument from design.", "Newman argued from conscience."]),
        PQ("Explain Aquinas's argument from contingency and give one criticism. (Model answer.)",
           "Things in the world come into being and pass away, so they are contingent: they might not have existed. If everything were contingent, then at some time nothing would have existed, and nothing could exist now. But something exists. So there must be at least one necessary being that does not depend on another for its existence, and this is God. Criticism: Russell says that explaining each thing does not require an explanation of the whole, and that the universe may be a brute fact; the argument may commit the fallacy of composition. Reply: Leibniz and Copleston say that the demand for an explanation of the whole is reasonable."),
        PQ("Outline Paley's design argument and Hume's main criticism. (Model answer.)",
           "Paley: a watch has parts that work together for a purpose, so it must have a maker; living things such as the eye show greater complexity, so they must have a designer. Hume's criticism: the analogy with a machine is weak; the universe may be more like an organism; we have not seen worlds made; the argument at most gives a limited designer; the world has disorder and suffering. Evolution (Darwin) later gave a different account of apparent design. Evaluation: the argument has force as an explanation of order in the laws of nature rather than in biology."),
        PQ("'The moral argument is more convincing than the cosmological argument.' Discuss. (Plan.)",
           "For: moral experience is universal; Kant's version does not claim to be a proof but shows a rational basis for faith; the cosmological argument has to deal with infinite regress and the fallacy of composition. Against: the moral argument faces the Euthyphro dilemma, evolutionary accounts of morality and the claim that morality can be objective without God; the cosmological argument speaks to the question of why anything exists at all. Evaluate: neither is a proof; each can be seen as supporting a reasonable belief. Give your own judgement with reasons."),
        TIP("Exam link: Philosophy of Religion is a Paper 3 section (Section 4.1 in the Board's report, which mentions the attributes of God, Kant, Aristotle, the Five Ways and death). The report says the Five Ways question was well known but misunderstood. Learn the names (motion, efficient causes, contingency), explain each as a step-by-step argument and give one criticism."),
        SUM("A priori arguments use reason alone; a posteriori arguments start from experience", "Ontological (Anselm, Descartes): from the idea of God; objections: Gaunilo, Kant", "Cosmological (Aquinas's first three ways; Leibniz; kalam): from the world's existence; objections: Hume, Russell, regress", "Teleological (Aquinas, Paley): from order; objections: Hume, Darwin, the problem of evil", "Moral (Kant, Newman): from duty and conscience; objections: Euthyphro, evolution, Freud", "No argument 'proves' God: weigh strengths and weaknesses"),
    ],
))


 # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Paper 1 
LESSONS.append(lesson(     module=MX, topic="Paper 1",     title="Paper 1: multiple choice on the fundamental concepts of the world religions",     slug="rsl2-exam-paper-1",     minutes=20,     summary="What Paper 1 contains (50 multiple-choice questions, about 34%), how to revise key terms across religions, how to eliminate wrong options, and a plan for time and accuracy.",     objectives=[         "State the structure of Paper 1 from the Board's 2023 report and what it tests",         "Build a revision plan of key terms, names and ideas for each module",         "Use elimination and the traps of religious studies questions (similar terms, overstatements, mixed religions)",         "Manage time and answer sheet accuracy in a practice paper",     ],     key_concepts=["Paper 1", "Multiple choice", "Key terms", "Elimination", "Comparative study", "Distractor"],     blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Paper 1 is the first of three papers for Religious Studies (0785). The Board's 2023 report describes it as 50 multiple-choice questions on the fundamental concepts of the five world religions as set by the syllabus, with a maximum raw mark of 50 and a weighting of about 34 per cent of the examination. Papers 2 and 3 carry about 33 per cent each. This means Paper 1 is worth as much as one of the long written papers, even though each question is short."),
        T("Think of Paper 1 as a test of **breadth**. You cannot choose a section as you can in Papers 2 and 3: any part of the syllabus may be asked. A Lower Sixth student should therefore revise every module, including the ones that seem less likely to appear in the long papers. The Board's recommendation is to study widely and to make comparisons between religions, because this gives a better chance on Paper 1."),
        T("Remember the Board's papers draw on the whole syllabus; this course covers the Lower Sixth modules (Old Testament, the Synoptic problem, Gospel theology and the alternative track). Your school may add other religions, for example Islam and African Traditional Religion, which the Board's report names as sections of Paper 2 and Paper 3. Check your teacher's list."),
        H("What the questions test"),
        KEY("Types of question", "Most Paper 1 items fit one of these.", cards=[             ["Recall", "Who, what, where, when: a name, a term, a place, a date or the right order of events."],             ["Definition", "What does a term mean (for example covenant, Logos, Kingdom of God, apologist)?"],             ["Distinguish", "Tell similar ideas apart (Messiah and Son of God; Nicaea and Chalcedon; J and E sources)."],             ["Apply or interpret", "Read a short description or passage and identify the idea, the Gospel or the scholar."],         ]),
        H("A revision plan"),
        T("Make a one-page sheet for each module with four headings: **terms** (with a one-line meaning), **names** (people, scholars and places), **dates and sequences** and **contrasts** (pairs that are often confused). For example, in the Gospel module the contrasts might be Synoptics and John, Son of God and Son of Man, dunamis and semeion. Test yourself by covering the answers. Repeat the sheets a little each day rather than all at once, because the spacing helps memory."),
        T("Use the practice questions in this platform in two ways: first by topic, to check your understanding; and later in a timed mock, to practise speed. When you get a question wrong, read the explanation of the wrong option as well as the right one, because the wrong options show the confusions that examiners use."),
        H("Answering technique"),
        D("**Elimination** means crossing out options that are wrong and choosing from the remaining ones. A distractor is a wrong option that looks right to someone with a common confusion.", title="Definition"),
        T("Read the question stem completely before you look at the options. Underline words like **not**, **except**, **only**, **always** and **main**. Options that use absolute words such as 'always' or 'only' are often wrong, because religious practice and belief have many exceptions, but do not assume this: check against what you know. In a question about one religion or one Gospel, check that the option is about that religion or Gospel, because an option may be true in another context (a correct statement about Matthew may be wrong as an answer about Luke)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: eliminating options", intro="Question (written for practice): Which of the following is a feature of the Gospel of John, but not of Mark?\nA. The parable of the Sower\nB. The 'I am' sayings with a picture, such as the bread of life\nC. The Temple cleansing\nD. The baptism of Jesus.", steps=[             S("Check A", text="The parable of the Sower is in Mark (chapter 4). Not John. Eliminate."),             S("Check C", text="The Temple cleansing is in Mark (and in John). It cannot distinguish them. Eliminate."),             S("Check D", text="Mark describes the baptism; John only alludes to John the Baptist's testimony. Eliminate."),             S("Answer", text="B. The picture 'I am' sayings (bread of life, light of the world and so on) are distinctive of John."),         ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a term that is easily confused", intro="Question (written for practice): Which statement about the Council of Chalcedon (451) is correct?\nA. It condemned Arius.\nB. It defined Jesus Christ as one person in two natures.\nC. It produced the Edict of Toleration.\nD. It chose the books of the New Testament.", steps=[             S("Check A", text="Arius was condemned at Nicaea (325). Eliminate."),             S("Check C", text="The toleration edicts were 311 (Galerius) and 313 (Milan). Eliminate."),             S("Check D", text="The canon was settled over a long period, not by Chalcedon. Eliminate."),             S("Answer", text="B."),         ]),
        H("Time and accuracy"),
        T("The Board's report does not state the time of Paper 1; check your examination timetable. In our practice mock we use 90 minutes (a KKEVO practice setting), which gives time to read carefully and check. Aim to answer every question; read the instructions on the paper about how marks are given. If you must guess, first remove the options you know are wrong. If you finish early, return to the questions you marked and check the stem and the answer sheet. Shade the answer clearly and make sure the number on the answer sheet matches the number of the question."),
        MISTAKE("Choosing the first option that sounds familiar", "Many wrong options are true statements about something else. Always ask: does this answer the question that was asked? Check the name, the religion and the book."),
        MISTAKE("Revising only the sections you like", "Paper 1 draws on the whole syllabus. The questions for the Old Testament and the Gospels are as likely as those for any section you enjoy, so spread your revision."),
        QUIZ("What does the Board's 2023 report say about Paper 1 of Religious Studies?", ["It has 18 essay questions", "It has 50 multiple-choice questions and about 34 per cent of the examination", "It has 36 structural questions", "It is a practical paper"], 1,              "Paper 1 is multiple choice (50 questions), about 34 per cent. Paper 2 has 18 essay questions and Paper 3 has 36 structural questions.", hint="Which paper is multiple choice?",              feedback=["That describes Paper 2.", "", "That describes Paper 3.", "Religious Studies has no practical paper."]),
        QUIZ("Which approach is best for a question that says 'Which of the following is NOT...'?", ["Choose the first true statement", "Check each option and choose the one that is false", "Choose the longest option", "Leave it blank"], 1,              "A negative question asks for the one false or unrelated option, so check all of them.", hint="NOT reverses the question.",              feedback=["That would give a true statement, the opposite of what is asked.", "", "Length is not a clue.", "Unanswered questions score nothing."]),
        PQ("Describe a method of revising key terms for Paper 1. (Model answer.)",            "Make a one-page sheet for each module with terms, names, dates and contrasts. For example: terms (Messiah, homoousios, covenant), names (Anselm, Irenaeus, Wrede), dates (325, 451), contrasts (Nicaea and Chalcedon). Cover the answers and test yourself every few days, mix topics, and return to wrong answers. Add comparisons between religions, as the Board recommends. Use timed mocks to practise speed."),
        PQ("Explain how to eliminate wrong options in a multiple-choice question on the Gospels. (Model answer.)",            "Read the stem and underline the key words (which Gospel, which title, 'not'). For each option ask whether it is true and whether it answers this question. Remove those that belong to another Gospel (for example a parable in Mark when the question asks about John) or another religion, or that are too absolute. If two remain, compare them with what you know of the text. Choose and move on."),
        PQ("List three habits that cost marks in Paper 1 and how to avoid them. (Model answer.)",            "(1) Not reading the stem fully: underline the key words. (2) Choosing a true statement that does not answer the question: check the name and the context. (3) Spending too long on one item: mark it, move on and return. Also check that the answer sheet matches the question number."),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's report says Paper 1 tests the fundamental concepts of the world religions in the syllabus, and recommends comparative study. The practice mock for Lower Sixth in this course uses 50 questions across all five modules; take it under timed conditions and then study the topic scores on the results page."),
        SUM("Paper 1: 50 multiple-choice questions, maximum 50 marks, about 34 per cent (Board's 2023 report)", "It covers fundamental concepts across the syllabus, so revise every module", "Revision sheet per module: terms, names, dates, contrasts", "Technique: read the stem, underline the key words, eliminate, check the context", "Practise a timed mock (90 minutes is a KKEVO practice setting) and review every wrong option", "Check the exact format and time with your teacher"),     ], )) 

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. Paper 2
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MX, topic="Paper 2",
    title="Paper 2: choosing a section and writing four strong essays",
    slug="rsl2-exam-paper-2",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The structure of Paper 2 (18 essay questions in three sections of six, four answers from one section), how to choose a section, how to read the command words, and how to plan an essay that explains significance rather than telling the story.",
    objectives=[
        "State the structure of Paper 2 from the Board's 2023 report",
        "Choose a section by checking that four questions in it can be answered well",
        "Read a question for its demand (explain, examine, justify, discuss) and plan a focused answer",
        "Avoid the faults the Board's report describes, especially narration instead of significance",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Paper 2", "Section choice", "Command word", "Significance", "Essay plan", "Evaluation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Paper 2 is an essay paper. The Board's 2023 report says it had 18 questions, divided into three sections of six, and that candidates were instructed to answer four questions, all from one section. The sections named in the report were the Old Testament (Section 1.1), Islam (Section 5.3) and African Traditional Religious Beliefs and Practices (Section 5.5). The report gives the weighting as about 33 per cent and the maximum raw mark as 100, so each of the four questions carries 25 marks."),
        T("Because you must choose one section, the first big decision is not how to write but **where**. If your school teaches the Old Testament in the Lower Sixth, as in this course, you will be able to choose it. Check with your teacher which sections your school covers."),
        H("Choosing a section"),
        KEY("Three tests for a section", "Do this in the first five minutes, and do not change once you start.", cards=[
            ["Count", "Find four questions in the section that you could answer with at least four developed points each. Do not choose a section because it has one favourite question."],
            ["Spread", "Check that the four you choose are not on the same narrow topic, so that you do not run out of different ideas."],
            ["Order", "Begin with the question you know best, but keep the time for the others the same."],
        ]),
        T("The report gives some idea of what is popular: in the Old Testament section the questions on the patriarchs (Jacob), the Exodus, the Judges, the confederacy and Saul, and David were all attempted, and the Jacob question was the most popular. Do not use this to guess the paper, since each year's questions differ. Use it to see what examiners ask for."),
        H("Read the demand"),
        D("The **command word** tells you what to do with the topic. **Explain**: give reasons. **Examine**: look closely and weigh the aspects. **Justify**: give reasons that support a claim. **Discuss**: give several sides with evidence and a conclusion. **Describe**: give the main features without long reasoning.", title="Command words"),
        T("The Board's comments on the Old Testament questions are clear. In the question on the **Exodus**, candidates were expected to show **why the event was central** to Israel's life, and many only narrated the story. In the question on the **Judges**, candidates were asked for the religious and political roles of the leaders, and many could not name them or show their strengths. In the question on the **confederacy and Saul** they were to show how the tribes were bound together and how that led to the monarchy, but many wrote only on Saul's rejection. In the question on **David** they were asked to explain his weakness and also the claim that he was a man after God's heart, so that both sides were needed. In the Islam section the question on Ramadan asked for the importance of the month, and many only described what happens. This is the **same fault** in every section: describing instead of answering."),
        MISTAKE("Telling the story when the question asks for significance", "When a question says 'examine the importance' or 'explain the significance', state the importance in the first sentence and prove it with two or three points. A short summary of the event is enough: spend the answer on what it meant for the people, for the covenant, for their worship or for later history."),
        H("Planning a 25-mark essay"),
        T("Spend about three to five minutes on a plan for each question. Write: the **demand** (one line), the **thesis** (your answer in one sentence), four or five **points** with an example or text for each, and a **conclusion**. The syllabus items in this course (for example the Exodus, the covenants, the monarchy) can all be planned in this way. Keep to the focus: if the question is on the confederacy and the monarchy, a paragraph on the sin of Saul belongs in your answer only if it helps to show the transition."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a plan on the Exodus", intro="Question (written for practice): 'Explain why the Exodus is central to the religion of Israel.'", steps=[
            S("Demand", text="Explain why: give reasons for the centrality, not a story."),
            S("Thesis", text="The Exodus is the founding act of Israel's relationship with God and shapes covenant, worship and identity."),
            S("Point 1", text="God freed a people from slavery: the basis of the claim that Yahweh acts in history; the opening of the Ten Commandments refers to it."),
            S("Point 2", text="It led to the covenant at Sinai: liberation before law."),
            S("Point 3", text="Passover remembers it each year, and the people were taught to tell it to their children."),
            S("Point 4", text="Later prophets and psalms recall it as a model of salvation (for example in the exile)."),
            S("Conclusion", text="Because of this the Exodus is the central memory of Israel. A short note: scholars debate the date and historicity (see the lesson on the Exodus)."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a plan on David", intro="Question (written for practice): 'David was a flawed king, yet he is remembered as a man after God's heart.' Discuss.", steps=[
            S("Demand", text="Discuss: both parts, with a judgement."),
            S("Flaws", text="Adultery with Bathsheba and the death of Uriah (2 Samuel 11); a census (2 Samuel 24); family troubles."),
            S("Strengths", text="United the tribes, captured Jerusalem, brought the ark to the city, received the promise of a dynasty (2 Samuel 7)."),
            S("Why he is a man after God's heart", text="He repented when confronted by Nathan (2 Samuel 12) and accepted the consequences; Psalm 51 is linked to this tradition."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The tradition does not claim that David was perfect; it shows him as a king who responded to God and who is the pattern for later hopes of a Messiah."),
        ]),
        H("Writing and time"),
        T("Divide your time equally. If the paper has four answers, take care that the fourth does not become a short list because you spent too long on the first. Write in short paragraphs, each with a clear opening sentence. Name texts and places only when you are sure. If you are not certain of a reference, give the event without the number: 'in the Court History of David' is better than a wrong verse. Define the key term in your introduction, and write a conclusion that answers the question."),
        MISTAKE("Choosing a section for one popular question and then struggling with the rest", "Four questions are needed from one section. Check all six before you decide, and do not pick a section because of one title that looks familiar."),
        QUIZ("According to the Board's 2023 report, how many Paper 2 questions must a candidate answer?", ["Two from each section", "Four, all from one section", "Six, one from each of six sections", "Eighteen"], 1,
             "Candidates answered four questions, choosing all from one section of six.", hint="Think of 'one section'.",
             feedback=["That is not the instruction in the report.", "", "Six questions make up each section; the paper has three sections.", "There are 18 questions in the paper, not 18 answers."]),
        QUIZ("A question says 'Examine the importance of the Exodus to Israel.' What should most of your answer do?", ["Retell the plagues and the crossing in detail", "Explain what the Exodus meant for Israel's faith, covenant and worship", "List the Egyptian kings", "Describe the journey in the wilderness"], 1,
             "The demand is the importance; a story is background only.", hint="Which word is the command word?",
             feedback=["Retelling is the fault the report describes.", "", "The names of the kings are not what is asked.", "The journey can be mentioned only if it supports the point."]),
        PQ("Explain how you would choose a section in Paper 2. (Model answer.)",
           "Read all six questions in each section you have studied. Count the questions you could answer with four or more developed points. Choose the section where you can answer four with reasonable depth, not the one with a single favourite question. Check that the four questions do not repeat the same material. Decide within five minutes and begin with your strongest question."),
        PQ("Plan an answer to: 'Examine the role of the judges in Israel.' (Model answer.)",
           "Demand: examine, with the religious and political roles. Thesis: the judges were charismatic leaders raised up by God to save the tribes. Religious role: call the people back to Yahweh, as in the cycle of the book of Judges (sin, oppression, cry, deliverer, rest). Political and military role: deliver tribes from neighbours such as Midianites (Gideon) and Philistines (Samson). Name leaders: Deborah, Gideon, Jephthah, Samson. Limits: local, temporary leadership; no central government, leading to the call for a king. Conclusion: the judges show the strengths and limits of the confederacy."),
        PQ("Why do essays on 'the importance of a festival' often lose marks, and how do you fix this? (Model answer.)",
           "They often describe the ritual steps in order. The question asks for importance, so state what the festival means (for example, in Judaism Passover remembers the Exodus), what it does for the community (unity, memory, worship) and how it connects to belief. Use two or three examples of practice only to prove the meaning."),
        TIP("Exam link: this course covers the Lower Sixth part of the Old Testament section of Paper 2 (Pentateuch and early Israel, the monarchy, and the Temple and festivals). Practise by writing a plan in five minutes for each topic, then checking it against the lesson."),
        SUM("Paper 2: 18 essay questions, three sections of six, answer four from one section, about 33 per cent (Board's 2023 report)", "Sections in 2023: Old Testament, Islam, African Traditional Religious Beliefs and Practices", "Choose the section where you can answer four questions well", "Read the command word: explain, examine, justify, discuss", "The report's main complaint: narration instead of significance", "Plan for 3 to 5 minutes; give each question equal time"),
    ],
))


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 12. Paper 3
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=MX, topic="Paper 3",
    title="Paper 3: structural questions on the Gospels, Acts, philosophy and ethics",
    slug="rsl2-exam-paper-3",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The structure of Paper 3 (36 structural questions in six sections, four answers from one section), how to answer a structural question in parts, what the Board's report says candidates did well and badly on Luke, John, Acts and Philosophy of Religion.",
    objectives=[
        "State the structure of Paper 3 and its sections from the Board's 2023 report",
        "Answer a structured question part by part, matching the length to the demand",
        "Use the Board's comments on Luke, John, Acts and Philosophy of Religion to improve your answers",
        "Plan revision of the sections taught in the Lower Sixth for Paper 3",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Paper 3", "Structural question", "Section choice", "Context", "Scripture reference", "Application and evaluation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Paper 3 has structural questions: each question is broken into parts that lead the candidate through a topic. The Board's 2023 report states that the paper had 36 questions in six sections of six questions each, and that candidates were to answer four questions from one section of their choice. The sections named were the Gospel of Luke (Section 2.1), John, the Fourth Gospel (Section 2.2), the Early Church as seen in Acts and First Corinthians (Section 2.3), Philosophy of Religion (Section 4.1), Ethics and Morality (Section 4.2) and the Historical Development of Islam (Section 6.0). The paper has a weighting of about 33 per cent and a maximum raw mark of 100."),
        T("The report also says the skills tested went from application and analysis up to synthesis and evaluation. In practice this means that the first parts of a question ask you to state or describe, and the last part asks you to explain, assess or give a reasoned judgement."),
        T("In the Lower Sixth, this course gives you the basis for Section 2.1 and 2.2 (the Gospels, through the Gospel theology module) and for Section 4.1 (through the arguments for God, if your school teaches the Philosophy of Religion track). Check with your teacher which sections your school offers."),
        H("How to answer a structural question"),
        KEY("A routine for each part", "Use it for every part, even short ones.", cards=[
            ["Read the marks", "Match the length of the answer to the marks shown beside the part. A part with two marks needs two points, not a paragraph."],
            ["Answer exactly that part", "Do not give the answer for the next part in advance; it is wasteful. Use the verbs: state, list, describe, explain, assess."],
            ["Use the text", "Name the passage or the person: for example, Luke 10 for the Good Samaritan, or John 11 for Lazarus. Do not give a verse number you are unsure about."],
            ["Finish with a judgement", "In the last part ('Assess', 'Explain the importance') give a clear point of view and the reason."],
        ]),
        H("What the Board's report says"),
        T("**Luke.** On the question about the authorship of Luke, many candidates could not give points to justify the traditional view that Luke wrote both the Gospel and Acts; the report points out that the two works are by the same author (see the shared preface, the same style and the continuing story). On Jesus' sending out of the disciples, candidates could explain the significance for evangelism but needed to say more about the situation in Palestine. On the attitude of Jesus to those to whom he ministered, the report notes that many failed to place their answers in the Palestinian context and instead wrote on the Pharisees. On the miracles, the question asked whether the recipients believed in them as genuine; the best answers took specific miracles and showed the power of God. On the Good Samaritan, candidates told the story too much and did not concentrate on love and discipleship. On the Emmaus story, the best answers explained how the risen Jesus gives hope and faith, and a few took Emmaus as a person."),
        T("**John.** The questions were less often chosen and the marks were generally lower. On the purposes and audience, candidates found it hard to give points on authorship, although they knew that Jews and Gentiles were among the readers. On the identity of Jesus at the start of the ministry, the report says that the key facts include John the Baptist's refusal to be called the Christ and his description of Jesus as the Son of God. On Jesus as the light of the world, the report expects the theme of spiritual blindness and says that candidates simply narrated the healings. On Lazarus, candidates did not bring out the significance of the sign (the glory of God and the first fruits of the resurrection). On the trials and crucifixion, candidates were expected to bring out the distinct trial scenes and the question 'was he the Messiah?', but wrote about the trials and then the crucifixion without linking them to the promised Saviour."),
        T("**Acts and First Corinthians.** The report praises answers on Pentecost (the coming of the Holy Spirit, power for mission and the start of the Church as an institution), on the leadership of Peter and Paul, and on the Jerusalem Council, where candidates were to explain how its decision helped the spread of the message among Gentiles. The question on the persecution of believers in Jerusalem expected the point that it helped the growth of the Church. On First Corinthians, candidates had to link Paul's teaching on marriage and on food offered to idols to the problems of the Corinthian church, and many did not make that link."),
        T("**Philosophy of Religion.** The report names questions on the attributes of God (omnipotence, omniscience, omnipresence), Kant's view of God, the goodness of God, the influence of Aristotle on Christianity (God as eternal and the cause of existence), death as a challenge, and the Five Ways. It says that candidates often misunderstood questions on Kant and on the Five Ways. The answer is to learn each argument step by step, as in the lesson on the arguments for the existence of God."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a structured question on Luke", intro="Question (written for practice): (a) State two parables found only in Luke. (b) Explain the teaching of the parable of the Good Samaritan. (c) Assess the view that Luke's Gospel is mainly about the poor and outcasts.", steps=[
            S("(a) State", text="Two parables found only in Luke: the Good Samaritan (Luke 10) and the Prodigal Son (Luke 15). Others: the Rich Fool (Luke 12). Two are enough."),
            S("(b) Explain", text="A lawyer asks 'who is my neighbour?'. The Samaritan, an outsider, helps the wounded man while a priest and a Levite pass by. Jesus turns the question: the neighbour is the one who shows mercy. The teaching is love that acts, and that crosses boundaries of race and religion; this is the heart of discipleship. Keep the story to two lines."),
            S("(c) Assess", text="For: Luke 4:16-21, 6:20-21, 7:22, 14, 15, 19 (Zacchaeus). Against: Luke also records the call of the rich to repent and includes the rich who respond, and the Gospel is for all nations. Judgement: the poor and outcasts are prominent, but the Gospel is not only about them."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a structured question on John", intro="Question (written for practice): (a) What does John 20:31 say about the purpose of the Gospel? (b) Explain how the healing of the man born blind illustrates the theme of light. (c) Assess the importance of the signs in John.", steps=[
            S("(a)", text="That readers may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing they may have life in his name."),
            S("(b)", text="Jesus says that he is the light of the world. The man gains physical sight, then spiritual sight when he confesses Jesus; the Pharisees claim to see but are spiritually blind (John 9)."),
            S("(c)", text="The signs point beyond themselves to the identity of Jesus and lead to belief (or rejection). Each is explained by a speech. They are selected by the author for his purpose. A judgement: they are central to John's way of showing who Jesus is."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Writing one long essay across all the parts", "In a structural question each part has its own marks and its own demand. If you answer part (c) inside part (a), you waste time and may leave (c) too short. Keep each answer separate and numbered."),
        MISTAKE("Giving the context that is not asked", "The report asks for situations in Palestine and the setting of the teaching. Include the setting, such as the Jewish background, in the part that asks for it, and only briefly elsewhere."),
        QUIZ("According to the Board's 2023 report, how many questions must a candidate answer in Paper 3?", ["Four, from one section of choice", "Six, one from each section", "Three, from different sections", "All thirty-six"], 0,
             "The paper has six sections of six questions; candidates answer four from a section of their choice.", hint="Think of 'a section of their choice'.",
             feedback=["", "There are six sections, but you do not answer one from each.", "That is not the instruction in the report.", "That would be the whole paper."]),
        QUIZ("Which is the best final-part answer to 'Assess the importance of the miracles of Jesus in Luke'?", ["A list of ten miracles", "A judgement supported by examples: signs of the Kingdom, faith of recipients, mixed responses", "A story of the feeding of the five thousand", "A statement that miracles are impossible"], 1,
             "An 'assess' question needs a reasoned judgement with evidence.", hint="Which command word asks for judgement?",
             feedback=["A list shows recall but gives no judgement.", "", "A story is narration.", "That is an opinion without argument and ignores the question."]),
        PQ("Explain how to prepare for Paper 3 questions on the Gospel of John. (Model answer.)",
           "Learn the purpose and audience (John 20:31; Jews and Gentiles), authorship views, the Prologue, the seven signs and the seven 'I am' sayings, the identity of Jesus in chapter 1 (John the Baptist's denial that he is the Christ), the light and blindness theme (John 9), Lazarus (John 11) and the trials and crucifixion. For each, prepare a one-paragraph explanation of what it shows about Jesus, with the passage named. Practise the three-step routine: state, explain, assess."),
        PQ("A part of a question is worth 5 marks and says 'Explain the significance of the Emmaus story'. How should you answer? (Model answer.)",
           "Write four or five short points: (1) the risen Jesus meets two disciples who do not recognise him; (2) he explains the scriptures to show that the Messiah had to suffer; (3) he is known in the breaking of bread; (4) the disciples return to Jerusalem with joy; (5) the story is a sign of hope and faith for later believers. Mention that Emmaus is a village. Avoid retelling the story at length."),
        PQ("Suggest a plan for revising Philosophy of Religion for Paper 3 in six weeks. (Model answer.)",
           "Week 1: the attributes of God (omnipotence, omniscience, omnipresence, goodness). Week 2: the ontological argument and objections. Week 3: the Five Ways (motion, efficient causes, contingency) and objections. Week 4: the design and moral arguments, Kant's view of God. Week 5: Aristotle's influence and death as a challenge. Week 6: practise structured questions under time and mark your answers against the lesson. Check the syllabus with your teacher."),
        TIP("Exam link: the section you choose should match the track your school teaches. Practise for Luke and John from the Gospel theology module, and for Philosophy of Religion from the lesson on the arguments for God if that is your track."),
        SUM("Paper 3: 36 structural questions, six sections of six, four answers from one section of choice, about 33 per cent (Board's 2023 report)", "Sections: Luke, John, Acts and First Corinthians, Philosophy of Religion, Ethics and Morality, Historical Development of Islam", "Answer each part separately, match length to marks, use named texts", "Board's comments: place Luke in its Palestinian setting; explain, do not narrate; John on spiritual blindness and Lazarus; Kant and the Five Ways step by step", "Final parts ask for a reasoned judgement", "Check your school's track and sections"),
    ],
))

